{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04373","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04373","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04373","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadi","familyName":"Nekoei","name":"Nekoei, Hadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Jaiswal","name":"Jaiswal, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrice","familyName":"Bechard","name":"Bechard, Patrice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oleh","familyName":"Shliazhko","name":"Shliazhko, Oleh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Orlando Marquez","familyName":"Ayala","name":"Ayala, Orlando Marquez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathieu","familyName":"Reymond","name":"Reymond, Mathieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Massimo","familyName":"Caccia","name":"Caccia, Massimo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Drouin","name":"Drouin, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarath","familyName":"Chandar","name":"Chandar, Sarath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Lacoste","name":"Lacoste, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JEF-Hinter: Leveraging Offline Knowledge for Improving Web Agents Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:34:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:07:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-20T19:34:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:04:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) agents perform well in sequential decision-making tasks, but improving them on unfamiliar domains often requires costly online interactions or fine-tuning on large expert datasets. These strategies are impractical for closed-source models and expensive for open-source ones, with risks of catastrophic forgetting. Offline trajectories offer reusable knowledge, yet demonstration-based methods struggle because raw traces are long, noisy, and tied to specific tasks. We present Just-in-time Episodic Feedback Hinter (JEF-Hinter), an agentic system that distills offline traces into compact, context-aware hints. A zooming mechanism highlights decisive steps in long trajectories, capturing both strategies and pitfalls. Unlike prior methods, JEF-Hinter leverages both successful and failed trajectories, extracting guidance even when only failure data is available, while supporting parallelized hint generation and benchmark-independent prompting. At inference, a retriever selects relevant hints for the current state, providing targeted guidance with transparency and traceability. Experiments on MiniWoB++, WorkArena-L1, and WebArena-Lite show that JEF-Hinter consistently outperforms strong baselines, including human- and document-based hints."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04373","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03817","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03817","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03817","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Becker","name":"Becker, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niklas","familyName":"Freymuth","name":"Freymuth, Niklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serge","familyName":"Thilges","name":"Thilges, Serge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Otto","name":"Otto, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerhard","familyName":"Neumann","name":"Neumann, Gerhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TROLL: Trust Regions improve Reinforcement Learning for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T14:14:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:35:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-31T13:36:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:41:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-23T18:54:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T02:45:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning (RL) with PPO-like clip objectives has become the standard choice for reward-based fine-tuning of large language models (LLMs). Although recent work has explored improved estimators of advantages and normalization, the clipping mechanism itself has remained untouched. Originally introduced as a proxy for principled KL-based trust regions, clipping is a crude approximation that often causes unstable updates and suboptimal performance. We replace the clip objective with a novel discrete differentiable trust region projection, which provides principled token-level KL constraints. The projection operates on a sparse subset of the model's most important token logits to balance computational cost and projection effectiveness. Our approach, Trust Region Optimization for Large Language models (TROLL), serves as a direct replacement for PPO-like clipping during training and does not alter the model's inference behavior. Across mathematical reasoning and code generation tasks, model families, as well as advantage-estimation methods, TROLL consistently outperforms PPO-like clipping in terms of training speed, stability, and final success rates."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03817","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:59:09Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:59:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03346","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03346","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03346","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Chiesa","name":"Chiesa, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerald Q.","familyName":"Maguire","name":"Maguire, Gerald Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dejan","familyName":"Kostic","name":"Kostic, Dejan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"KVComm: Enabling Efficient LLM Communication through Selective KV Sharing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T16:01:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-12T09:38:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:38:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-22T15:15:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:52:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in multi-agent systems, where effective inter-model communication is crucial. Existing communication protocols either rely on natural language, incurring high inference costs and information loss, or on hidden states, which suffer from information concentration bias and inefficiency. To address these limitations, we propose KVComm, a novel communication framework that enables efficient communication between LLMs through selective sharing of KV pairs. KVComm leverages the rich information encoded in the KV pairs while avoiding the pitfalls of hidden states. We introduce a KV layer-wise selection strategy based on attention importance scores with a Gaussian prior to identify the most informative KV pairs for communication. Extensive experiments across diverse tasks and model pairs demonstrate that KVComm achieves comparable performance to the upper-bound method, which directly merges inputs to one model without any communication, while transmitting as few as 30\\% of layers' KV pairs. Our study highlights the potential of KV pairs as an effective medium for inter-LLM communication, paving the way for scalable and efficient multi-agent systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03346","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:41Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03313","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03313","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03313","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anirudh","familyName":"Subramanyam","name":"Subramanyam, Anirudh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert L.","familyName":"Grossman","name":"Grossman, Robert L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling Laws Revisited: Modeling the Role of Data Quality in Language Model Pretraining"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T22:45:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-23T17:54:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T02:42:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scaling laws for language model training traditionally characterize how performance scales with model size and dataset volume. Prior work has explored architecture variants and data treatments such as dataset filtering and noise injection in language model pretraining; however, these studies have not formalized data quality within a principled scaling law. We introduce a dimensionless data-quality parameter Q, and propose a quality-aware scaling law extending the Chinchilla framework to predict loss as a joint function of model size, data volume, and data quality. The law is motivated by an effective-sample-size and information-theoretic view of noisy or redundant corpora, and it admits two practical estimators for Q: (i) a corruption rate proxy and (ii) a deficiency measure. Through synthetic experiments in neural machine translation and autoregressive modeling -- where we systematically control data quality via multiple levels of noise injection variation -- we show that loss scales predictably with data quality and that higher-quality data can substantially reduce model size and hence compute requirements. Our results demonstrate a sublinear decay of effective data with quality and robustness to moderate data corruption; out-of-sample evaluations further validate the predictive form of the law. Unlike prior empirical analyses, our work establishes an explicit, generalizable law for data quality, offering concrete guidance for balancing data curation effort and model scale in large-scale pretraining."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03313","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:53Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04694","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04694","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04694","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas","familyName":"Bandarkar","name":"Bandarkar, Lucas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chenyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohsen","familyName":"Fayyaz","name":"Fayyaz, Mohsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junlin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Junlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanyun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Nanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multilingual Routing in Mixture-of-Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T11:09:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:27:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-17T19:00:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-19T01:03:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures have become the key to scaling modern LLMs, yet little is understood about how their sparse routing dynamics respond to multilingual data. In this work, we analyze expert routing patterns using parallel multilingual datasets and present highly interpretable layer-wise phenomena. We find that MoE models route tokens in language-specific ways in the early and late decoder layers but exhibit significant cross-lingual routing alignment in middle layers, mirroring parameter-sharing trends observed in dense LLMs. In particular, we reveal a clear, strong correlation between a model's performance in a given language and how similarly its tokens are routed to English in these layers. Extending beyond correlation, we explore inference-time interventions that induce higher cross-lingual routing alignment. We introduce a method that steers the router by promoting middle-layer task experts frequently activated in English, and it successfully increases multilingual performance. These 1-2% gains are remarkably consistent across two evaluation tasks, three models, and 15+ languages, especially given that these simple interventions override routers of extensively trained, state-of-the-art LLMs. In comparison, interventions outside of the middle layers or targeting multilingual-specialized experts only yield performance degradation. Altogether, we present numerous findings that explain how MoEs process non-English text and demonstrate that generalization is limited by the model's ability to leverage language-universal experts in all languages."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026, In The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations, 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04694","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:20:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:20:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-19T03:01:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03269","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03269","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03269","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wendi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wendi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changdae","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Changdae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharon","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sharon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"General Exploratory Bonus for Optimistic Exploration in RLHF"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T04:54:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T14:34:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:54:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T20:29:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:06:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-17T07:26:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-18T01:25:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Optimistic exploration is central to improving sample efficiency in reinforcement learning with human feedback, yet existing exploratory bonus methods to incentivize exploration often fail to realize optimism. We provide a theoretical analysis showing that current formulations, under KL or $α$-divergence regularization, unintentionally bias exploration toward high-probability regions of the reference model, thereby reinforcing conservative behavior instead of promoting discovery of uncertain regions. To address this pitfall, we introduce the General Exploratory Bonus (GEB), a novel theoretical framework that provably satisfies the optimism principle. GEB counteracts divergence-induced bias via reference-dependent reward regulation and unifies prior heuristic bonuses as special cases, while extending naturally across the full $α$-divergence family. Empirically, GEB consistently outperforms baselines on alignment tasks across multiple divergence settings and large language model backbones. These results demonstrate that GEB offers both a principled and practical solution for optimistic exploration in RLHF."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03269","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-18T02:57:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04398","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04398","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04398","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Buyun","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Buyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangzu","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Liangzu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinqi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jinqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Darshan","familyName":"Thaker","name":"Thaker, Darshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kwan Ho Ryan","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Kwan Ho Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"René","familyName":"Vidal","name":"Vidal, René","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SECA: Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks for Eliciting LLM Hallucinations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T23:44:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:08:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-30T19:12:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:55:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-15T20:24:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-17T02:06:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in high-risk domains. However, state-of-the-art LLMs often exhibit hallucinations, raising serious concerns about their reliability. Prior work has explored adversarial attacks to elicit hallucinations in LLMs, but these methods often rely on unrealistic prompts, either by inserting nonsensical tokens or by altering the original semantic intent. Consequently, such approaches provide limited insight into how hallucinations arise in real-world settings. In contrast, adversarial attacks in computer vision typically involve realistic modifications to input images. However, the problem of identifying realistic adversarial prompts for eliciting LLM hallucinations remains largely underexplored. To address this gap, we propose Semantically Equivalent and Coherent Attacks (SECA), which elicit hallucinations via realistic modifications to the prompt that preserve its meaning while maintaining semantic coherence. Our contributions are threefold: (i) we formulate finding realistic attacks for hallucination elicitation as a constrained optimization problem over the input prompt space under semantic equivalence and coherence constraints; (ii) we introduce a constraint-preserving zeroth-order method to effectively search for adversarial yet feasible prompts; and (iii) we demonstrate through experiments on open-ended multiple-choice question answering tasks that SECA achieves higher attack success rates while incurring almost no semantic equivalence or semantic coherence errors compared to existing methods. SECA highlights the sensitivity of both open-source and commercial gradient-inaccessible LLMs to realistic and plausible prompt variations. Code is available at https://github.com/Buyun-Liang/SECA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025. Code is available at https://github.com/Buyun-Liang/SECA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04398","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:32Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:13:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:46:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03777","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03777","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03777","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Divij","familyName":"Handa","name":"Handa, Divij","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mihir","familyName":"Parmar","name":"Parmar, Mihir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aswin","familyName":"RRV","name":"RRV, Aswin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Nayem","familyName":"Uddin","name":"Uddin, Md Nayem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Palangi","name":"Palangi, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chitta","familyName":"Baral","name":"Baral, Chitta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GuidedSampling: Steering LLMs Towards Diverse Candidate Solutions at Inference-Time"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T11:02:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:32:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-14T01:00:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-17T01:15:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Repeated Sampling (RS) is a simple inference-time algorithm that has been shown to improve model performance on complex tasks. Although it is an effective way of scaling inference time, it often struggles to generate diverse solution candidates, frequently relying on the same underlying approach to solve the problem and thus producing redundant samples. To address this limitation, we propose a new inference algorithm, GuidedSampling, which decouples the exploration and generation phases during inference, increasing diversity of generated candidate solutions. The exploration phase identifies multiple concepts that can be utilized to solve the problem, while the generation phase applies a specific concept to provide final solution candidates. We first define the theoretical bounds of GuidedSampling and then empirically demonstrate that it improves the performance of base model at pass@50 by on an average ~21.6% across various benchmarks compared to RS. Furthermore, models trained on trajectories of GuidedSampling exhibit substantial performance improvements at pass@5 by on an average ~9.7%, compared to models trained on traditional RS. Additionally, models trained with GuidedSampling increases the average number of concepts per instance (1.67 -\u0026gt; 3.03), yielding a diverse set of candidates than traditional RS."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03777","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:58:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:58:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:46:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03669","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03669","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03669","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenlong","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Wenlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yushu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boying","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Boying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danica J.","familyName":"Sutherland","name":"Sutherland, Danica J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christos","familyName":"Thrampoulidis","name":"Thrampoulidis, Christos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Token Hidden Reward: Steering Exploration-Exploitation in Group Relative Deep Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T04:49:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-20T21:59:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T22:16:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-20T21:59:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T23:53:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:11:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-15T01:21:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-17T01:43:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards has significantly advanced the reasoning capabilities of large language models, yet how to explicitly steer training toward exploration or exploitation remains an open problem. We introduce Token Hidden Reward (THR), a token-level metric that quantifies each token's influence on the likelihood of correct responses under Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO). We find that training dynamics are dominated by a small subset of tokens with high absolute THR values. Most interestingly, tokens with positive THR strengthen confidence in correct outputs, thus favoring exploitation, while tokens with negative THR preserve probability mass for alternative outputs, enabling exploration. This insight suggests a natural intervention: a THR-guided reweighting algorithm that modulates GRPO's learning signals to explicitly bias training toward exploitation or exploration. We validate the efficacy of this algorithm on diverse math reasoning benchmarks. By amplifying tokens with positive THR value and weakening negative ones, our algorithm improves greedy-decoding accuracy, favoring exploitation. The reverse strategy yields consistent gains in Pass@K accuracy, favoring exploration. We further demonstrate that our algorithm integrates seamlessly with other RL objectives such as GSPO and generalizes across architectures including Llama. These findings establish THR as a principled and fine-grained mechanism for dynamically controlling exploration and exploitation in RL-tuned LLMs, providing new tools for targeted fine-tuning in reasoning-intensive applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Full version of submission to 2nd AI for Math Workshop@ ICML 2025 (best paper)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03669","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:46:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03574","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03574","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03574","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehmet Onurcan","familyName":"Kaya","name":"Kaya, Mehmet Onurcan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Desmond","familyName":"Elliott","name":"Elliott, Desmond","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dim P.","familyName":"Papadopoulos","name":"Papadopoulos, Dim P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Test-Time Scaling for Small Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T23:49:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:14:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-16T15:56:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-17T02:42:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Small Vision-Language Models (VLMs) provide a computationally efficient alternative to larger models, at the cost of weaker generalization abilities and downstream task performance. These shortcomings could be addressed by test-time scaling techniques, but existing methods are typically computationally demanding, contradicting the resource-efficient design goals of small models. To address these limitations, we propose two novel and efficient test-time scaling strategies that leverage the model-internal features rather than external supervision: (i) Test-Time Augmentation (TTAug), which generates multiple augmented inputs and aggregates outputs at the token level without parameter updates, and (ii) Test-Time Adaptation (TTAdapt), which adapts model parameters during inference using consensus-based pseudolabels from TTAug. Through extensive experiments across nine benchmarks, we demonstrate consistent performance improvements while maintaining computational efficiency suitable for resource-constrained environments. The generality of our approach is demonstrated both within models at different scales and across different VLMs without additional tuning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026. Project Page: https://monurcan.github.io/efficient_test_time_scaling"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03574","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:53:13Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:53:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:46:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03272","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03272","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03272","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueqing","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xueqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Where to Add PDE Diffusion in Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T08:58:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-12T14:32:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:05:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-16T15:53:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-17T02:42:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers enable powerful content-based global routing via self-attention, but they lack an explicit local geometric prior along the sequence axis. As a result, the placement of locality-inducing modules in hybrid architectures has largely been empirical. We study a simple deterministic PDE diffusion layer implemented as one explicit Euler step of one-dimensional heat smoothing using a discrete Neumann Laplacian under a spectral stability constraint, and ask a structural question: where should diffusion be inserted relative to attention? Our central claim is that diffusion and attention generally do not commute, so inserting the same local operator before versus after attention leads to qualitatively different behaviors. We develop a three-layer operator-theoretic framework that (1) establishes unconditional guarantees for the diffusion subsystem, including spectral non-expansiveness and monotone Dirichlet-energy dissipation when the diffusion step size is smaller than one half, (2) derives compositional perturbation bounds linking insertion effects to representation roughness and downstream amplification, and (3) uses diffusion-attention non-commutativity as a diagnostic for structural double-mixing conflicts. Guided by theory, we evaluate seven insertion positions on the Long Range Arena benchmark. Early diffusion acts as effective pre-regularization, improving average accuracy by 4.1 percentage points when applied after embedding, while post-attention diffusion degrades performance by 2.5 percentage points, consistent with the predicted conflict. A multi-scale diffusion variant yields consistent gains under the same global stability constraint. Our analysis provides a general template for reasoning about local-global compositions in sequence models by separating provable guarantees, compositional bounds, and mechanistic diagnostics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03272","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:51Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:46:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03667","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03667","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03667","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessica Y.","familyName":"Bo","name":"Bo, Jessica Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Majeed","familyName":"Kazemitabaar","name":"Kazemitabaar, Majeed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengqing","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Mengqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Inzlicht","name":"Inzlicht, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashton","familyName":"Anderson","name":"Anderson, Ashton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Invisible Saboteurs: Sycophantic LLMs Mislead Novices in Problem-Solving Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T04:46:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:23:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-10T21:59:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-12T01:09:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sycophancy, the tendency of LLM-based chatbots to express excessive agreement with their users, even when inappropriate, is emerging as a significant risk in human-AI interactions. However, the extent to which this affects human-LLM collaboration in complex problem-solving tasks is not well quantified, especially among novices who are prone to misconceptions. We created two LLM chatbots, one with high sycophancy and one with low sycophancy, and conducted a within-subjects experiment (n=24) in the context of debugging machine learning models to investigate the effect of sycophancy on users' mental models, workflows, reliance behaviors, and perceptions of the chatbots. Our findings show that users of the high sycophancy chatbot were less likely to correct their misconceptions and spent more time over-relying on unhelpful LLM responses, leading them to significantly worse performance in the task. Despite these impaired outcomes, a majority of users were unable to detect the presence of excessive sycophancy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACM CHI conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems (2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03667","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:28Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-12T03:21:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04787","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04787","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04787","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zifan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaitao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Kaitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guosheng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Guosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ding","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Ding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyao","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Junyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohua","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaohua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongsheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cairong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Cairong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trade in Minutes! Rationality-Driven Agentic System for Quantitative Financial Trading"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:08:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:32:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-09T07:01:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:32:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) and agentic systems have shown exceptional decision-making capabilities, revealing significant potential for autonomic finance. Current financial trading agents predominantly simulate anthropomorphic roles that inadvertently introduce emotional biases and rely on peripheral information, while being constrained by the necessity for continuous inference during deployment. In this paper, we pioneer the harmonization of strategic depth in agents with the mechanical rationality essential for quantitative trading. Consequently, we present TiMi (Trade in Minutes), a rationality-driven multi-agent system that architecturally decouples strategy development from minute-level deployment. TiMi leverages specialized LLM capabilities of semantic analysis, code programming, and mathematical reasoning within a comprehensive policy-optimization-deployment chain. Specifically, we propose a two-tier analytical paradigm from macro patterns to micro customization, layered programming design for trading bot implementation, and closed-loop optimization driven by mathematical reflection. Extensive evaluations across 200+ trading pairs in stock and cryptocurrency markets empirically validate the efficacy of TiMi in stable profitability, action efficiency, and risk control under volatile market dynamics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04787","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:23:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:23:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-10T05:21:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03944","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03944","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03944","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiqing","familyName":"He","name":"He, Weiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianqi","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Tianqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijie","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Weijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Empirical Power of Goodness-of-Fit Tests in Watermark Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T21:07:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:43:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-05T21:10:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-09T01:07:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) raise concerns about content authenticity and integrity because they can generate human-like text at scale. Text watermarks, which embed detectable statistical signals into generated text, offer a provable way to verify content origin. Many detection methods rely on pivotal statistics that are i.i.d. under human-written text, making goodness-of-fit (GoF) tests a natural tool for watermark detection. However, GoF tests remain largely underexplored in this setting. In this paper, we systematically evaluate eight GoF tests across three popular watermarking schemes, using three open-source LLMs, two datasets, various generation temperatures, and multiple post-editing methods. We find that general GoF tests can improve both the detection power and robustness of watermark detectors. Notably, we observe that text repetition, common in low-temperature settings, gives GoF tests a unique advantage not exploited by existing methods. Our results highlight that classic GoF tests are a simple yet powerful and underused tool for watermark detection in LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 as a spotlight"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03944","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-09T03:01:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03999","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03999","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03999","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Yeh","name":"Yeh, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jwala","familyName":"Dhamala","name":"Dhamala, Jwala","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ousmane","familyName":"Dia","name":"Dia, Ousmane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharon","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sharon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LH-Deception: Simulating and Understanding LLM Deceptive Behaviors in Long-Horizon Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T02:18:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T15:30:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:58:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T12:03:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:43:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deception is a pervasive feature of human communication and an emerging concern in large language models (LLMs). While recent studies document instances of LLM deception, most evaluations remain confined to single-turn prompts and fail to capture the long-horizon interactions in which deceptive strategies typically unfold. We introduce a new simulation framework, LH-Deception, for a systematic, empirical quantification of deception in LLMs under extended sequences of interdependent tasks and dynamic contextual pressures. LH-Deception is designed as a multi-agent system: a performer agent tasked with completing tasks and a supervisor agent that evaluates progress, provides feedback, and maintains evolving states of trust. An independent deception auditor then reviews full trajectories to identify when and how deception occurs. We conduct extensive experiments across 11 frontier models, spanning both closed-source and open-source systems, and find that deception is model-dependent, increases with event pressure, and consistently erodes supervisor trust. Qualitative analyses further reveal emergent, long-horizon phenomena, such as ``chains of deception\", which are invisible to static, single-turn evaluations. Our findings provide a foundation for evaluating future LLMs in real-world, trust-sensitive contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03999","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:21:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03969","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03969","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03969","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengxiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chengxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isha","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Isha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weitong","familyName":"Ruan","name":"Ruan, Weitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gagandeep","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Gagandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Catastrophic is Your LLM? Certifying Risk in Conversation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T23:00:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-04T16:24:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T02:00:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T02:22:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:17:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) can produce catastrophic responses in conversational settings that pose serious risks to public safety and security. Existing evaluations often fail to fully reveal these vulnerabilities because they rely on fixed attack prompt sequences, lack statistical guarantees, and do not scale to the vast space of multi-turn conversations. In this work, we propose C$^3$LLM, a novel, principled statistical Certification framework for Catastrophic risks in multi-turn Conversation for LLMs that bounds the probability of an LLM generating catastrophic responses under multi-turn conversation distributions with statistical guarantees. We model multi-turn conversations as probability distributions over query sequences, represented by a Markov process on a query graph whose edges encode semantic similarity to capture realistic conversational flow, and quantify catastrophic risks using confidence intervals. We define several inexpensive and practical distributions--random node, graph path, and adaptive with rejection. Our results demonstrate that these distributions can reveal substantial catastrophic risks in frontier models, with certified lower bounds as high as 70% for the worst model, highlighting the urgent need for improved safety training strategies in frontier LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03969","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:59Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:21:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04670","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04670","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04670","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanhua","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Xuanhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runkai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Runkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weidong","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Weidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Multimodal Brain Encoding Model with Dynamic Subject-awareness Routing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T10:24:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:25:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T06:31:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:27:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-04T02:43:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-05T01:20:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Naturalistic fMRI encoding must handle multimodal inputs, shifting fusion styles, and pronounced inter-subject variability. We introduce AFIRE (Agnostic Framework for Multimodal fMRI Response Encoding), an agnostic interface that standardizes time-aligned post-fusion tokens from varied encoders, and MIND, a plug-and-play Mixture-of-Experts decoder with a subject-aware dynamic gating. Trained end-to-end for whole-brain prediction, AFIRE decouples the decoder from upstream fusion, while MIND combines token-dependent Top-K sparse routing with a subject prior to personalize expert usage without sacrificing generality. Experiments across multiple multimodal backbones and subjects show consistent improvements over strong baselines, enhanced cross-subject generalization, and interpretable expert patterns that correlate with content type. The framework offers a simple attachment point for new encoders and datasets, enabling robust, plug-and-improve performance for naturalistic neuroimaging studies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 4 figures, accepted by ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04670","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:20:09Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:20:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-05T03:21:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04217","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04217","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04217","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenlu","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Chenlu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiancan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiancan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leheng","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Leheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yancheng","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Yancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangnan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xiangnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MLLMEraser: Achieving Test-Time Unlearning in Multimodal Large Language Models through Activation Steering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T14:20:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:58:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T11:47:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:44:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T12:41:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-03T02:58:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across vision-language tasks, yet their large-scale deployment raises pressing concerns about memorized private data, outdated knowledge, and harmful content. Existing unlearning approaches for MLLMs typically adapt training-based strategies such as gradient ascent or preference optimization, but these methods are computationally expensive, irreversible, and often distort retained knowledge. In this work, we propose MLLMEraser, an input-aware, training-free framework for test-time unlearning. Our approach leverages activation steering to enable dynamic knowledge erasure without parameter updates. Specifically, we construct a multimodal erasure direction by contrasting adversarially perturbed, knowledge-recall image-text pairs with knowledge-erasure counterparts, capturing both textual and visual discrepancies. To prevent unnecessary interference, we further design an input-aware steering mechanism that adaptively determines when and how the erasure direction should be applied, preserving utility on retained knowledge while enforcing forgetting on designated content. Experiments on LLaVA-1.5 and Qwen-2.5-VL demonstrate that MLLMEraser consistently outperforms state-of-the-art MLLM unlearning baselines, achieving stronger forgetting performance with lower computational cost and minimal utility degradation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04217","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:40:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04347","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04347","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04347","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anindya Sundar","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Anindya Sundar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangjie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kangjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monowar","familyName":"Bhuyan","name":"Bhuyan, Monowar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unmasking Backdoors: An Explainable Defense via Gradient-Attention Anomaly Scoring for Pre-trained Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:15:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:05:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T11:17:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:45:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pre-trained language models have achieved remarkable success across a wide range of natural language processing (NLP) tasks, particularly when fine-tuned on large, domain-relevant datasets. However, they remain vulnerable to backdoor attacks, where adversaries embed malicious behaviors using trigger patterns in the training data. These triggers remain dormant during normal usage, but, when activated, can cause targeted misclassifications. In this work, we investigate the internal behavior of backdoored pre-trained encoder-based language models, focusing on the consistent shift in attention and gradient attribution when processing poisoned inputs; where the trigger token dominates both attention and gradient signals, overriding the surrounding context. We propose an inference-time defense that constructs anomaly scores by combining token-level attention and gradient information. Extensive experiments on text classification tasks across diverse backdoor attack scenarios demonstrate that our method significantly reduces attack success rates compared to existing baselines. Furthermore, we provide an interpretability-driven analysis of the scoring mechanism, shedding light on trigger localization and the robustness of the proposed defense."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages total (9 pages main text + 6 pages appendix + references), 16 figures. Preprint version; the final camera-ready version may differ. Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04347","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:17Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03370","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03370","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03370","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junde","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Junde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yapin","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yapin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijun","familyName":"Lang","name":"Lang, Lijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taoyong","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Taoyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guangyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiezhong","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Jiezhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pheng-Ann","familyName":"Heng","name":"Heng, Pheng-Ann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InstructPLM-mu: 1-Hour Fine-Tuning of ESM2 Beats ESM3 in Protein Mutation Predictions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T07:42:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T02:14:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:17:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-30T02:30:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:17:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal protein language models deliver strong performance on mutation-effect prediction, but training such models from scratch demands substantial computational resources. In this paper, we propose a fine-tuning framework called InstructPLM-mu and try to answer a question: \\textit{Can multimodal fine-tuning of a pretrained, sequence-only protein language model match the performance of models trained end-to-end? } Surprisingly, our experiments show that fine-tuning ESM2 with structural inputs can reach performance comparable to ESM3. To understand how this is achieved, we systematically compare three different feature-fusion designs and fine-tuning recipes. Our results reveal that both the fusion method and the tuning strategy strongly affect final accuracy, indicating that the fine-tuning process is not trivial. We hope this work offers practical guidance for injecting structure into pretrained protein language models and motivates further research on better fusion mechanisms and fine-tuning protocols."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03370","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:16Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03267","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03267","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03267","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianglong","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xianglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzhu","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Chengzhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiteng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaicheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kaicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotong","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Haotong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruobing","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Ruobing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingwu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xingwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yulun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PT$^2$-LLM: Post-Training Ternarization for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T03:01:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T05:42:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:28:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown impressive capabilities across diverse tasks, but their large memory and compute demands hinder deployment. Ternarization has gained attention as a promising compression technique, delivering substantial size reduction and high computational efficiency. However, its potential in the post-training quantization (PTQ) setting remains underexplored, due to the challenge of training-free parameter optimization and the quantization difficulty posed by outliers and dispersed weights. To address these issues, we propose PT$^2$-LLM, a post-training ternarization framework tailored for LLMs. At its core is an Asymmetric Ternary Quantizer equipped with a two-stage refinement pipeline: (1) Iterative Ternary Fitting (ITF), which alternates between optimal ternary grid construction and flexible rounding to minimize quantization error, and (2) Activation-aware Grid Alignment (AGA), which further refines the ternary grid to better match full-precision outputs. In addition, we propose a plug-and-play Structural Similarity-based Reordering (SSR) strategy that leverages inter-column structural similarity to ease quantization and mitigate outlier effects, further enhancing overall performance. Extensive experiments demonstrate that PT$^2$-LLM delivers competitive performance against state-of-the-art (SOTA) 2-bit PTQ methods with lower memory cost, while also accelerating both prefill and decoding to achieve end-to-end speedup. The code and models will be available at https://github.com/XIANGLONGYAN/PT2-LLM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03267","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03871","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03871","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03871","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oleg","familyName":"Filatov","name":"Filatov, Oleg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangtao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiangtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Ebert","name":"Ebert, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Kesselheim","name":"Kesselheim, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal Scaling Needs Optimal Norm"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T16:48:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:39:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T16:32:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T02:03:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite recent progress in optimal hyperparameter transfer under model and dataset scaling, no unifying explanatory principle has been established. For Adam and Scion optimizers, we discover that joint optimal scaling across model and dataset sizes is conditioned on a single invariant: the operator norm of the output layer. Across models with up to 1.3B parameters trained on up to 138B tokens, the optimal learning rate/batch size pair $(η^{\\ast}, B^{\\ast})$ consistently has the same operator norm value - a phenomenon we term norm transfer. This constant norm condition is necessary but not sufficient: while for each dataset size, multiple $(η, B)$ reach the optimal norm, only a unique $(η^{\\ast}, B^{\\ast})$ achieves the best loss. As a sufficient condition, we provide the first measurement of $(η^{\\ast}, B^{\\ast})$ scaling with dataset size for Scion, and find that the scaling rules are consistent with those of Adam. Tuning per-layer-group learning rates also improves model performance, with the output layer being the most sensitive and hidden layers benefiting from lower learning rates. We provide practical insights on norm-guided optimal scaling and release our Distributed Scion (Disco) implementation with logs from over two thousand runs to support research on LLM training dynamics at scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03871","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:00:32Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:00:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-28T03:24:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wengao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Wengao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianlei","familyName":"Shan","name":"Shan, Lianlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Thinking on the Fly: Test-Time Reasoning Enhancement via Latent Thought Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T12:50:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:56:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-07T17:43:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:57:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T21:10:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-18T01:08:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-26T14:30:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:31:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have shifted from explicit Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning to more efficient latent reasoning, where intermediate thoughts are represented as vectors rather than text. However, latent reasoning can be brittle on challenging, out-of-distribution tasks where robust reasoning is most critical. To overcome these limitations, we introduce Latent Thought Policy Optimization (LTPO), a parameter-free framework that enhances LLM reasoning entirely at test time, without requiring model parameter updates. LTPO treats intermediate latent \"thought\" vectors as dynamic parameters that are actively optimized for each problem instance. It employs an online policy gradient method guided by an intrinsic, confidence-based reward signal computed directly from the frozen LLM's own output distributions, eliminating the need for external supervision or expensive text generation during optimization. Extensive experiments on five reasoning benchmarks show that LTPO not only matches or surpasses strong baselines on standard tasks but also demonstrates remarkable robustness where others fail. Most notably, on highly challenging AIME benchmarks where existing latent reasoning baselines collapse to near-zero accuracy, LTPO delivers substantial improvements, showcasing a unique capability for complex reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:35:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03906","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03906","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03906","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ardalan","familyName":"Aryashad","name":"Aryashad, Ardalan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parsa","familyName":"Razmara","name":"Razmara, Parsa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin","familyName":"Mahjoub","name":"Mahjoub, Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyedarmin","familyName":"Azizi","name":"Azizi, Seyedarmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdi","familyName":"Salmani","name":"Salmani, Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arad","familyName":"Firouzkouhi","name":"Firouzkouhi, Arad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Filters to VLMs: Benchmarking Defogging Methods through Object Detection and Segmentation Performance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:05:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T12:26:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:25:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous driving perception systems are particularly vulnerable in foggy conditions, where light scattering reduces contrast and obscures fine details critical for safe operation. While numerous defogging methods exist, from handcrafted filters to learned restoration models, improvements in image fidelity do not consistently translate into better downstream detection and segmentation. Moreover, prior evaluations often rely on synthetic data, raising concerns about real-world transferability.\n We present a structured empirical study that benchmarks a comprehensive set of defogging pipelines, including classical dehazing filters, modern defogging networks, chained variants combining filters and models, and prompt-driven visual language image editing models applied directly to foggy images. To bridge the gap between simulated and physical environments, we evaluate these pipelines on both the synthetic Foggy Cityscapes dataset and the real-world Adverse Conditions Dataset with Correspondences (ACDC).\n We examine generalization by evaluating performance on synthetic fog and real-world conditions, assessing both image quality and downstream perception in terms of object detection mean average precision and segmentation panoptic quality. Our analysis identifies when defogging is effective, the impact of combining models, and how visual language models compare to traditional approaches. We additionally report qualitative rubric-based evaluations from both human and visual language model judges and analyze their alignment with downstream task metrics.\n Together, these results establish a transparent, task-oriented benchmark for defogging methods and identify the conditions under which pre-processing meaningfully improves autonomous perception in adverse weather.\n Project page: https://aradfir.github.io/filters-to-vlms-defogging-page/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at WACV 2026 Proceedings (Oral), 5th Workshop on Image, Video, and Audio Quality Assessment in Computer Vision, with a focus on VLM and Diffusion Models"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03906","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:26Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:35:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03437","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03437","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03437","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jairo","familyName":"Diaz-Rodriguez","name":"Diaz-Rodriguez, Jairo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mumin","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Mumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Consistent Kernel Change-Point Detection under m-Dependence for Text Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:57:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:06:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T18:36:37Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; This paper is withdrawn due to an error in the proof of Proposition 3, which is used to support Theorem 1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:46:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Kernel change-point detection (KCPD) has become a widely used tool for identifying structural changes in complex data. While existing theory establishes consistency under independence assumptions, real-world sequential data such as text exhibits strong dependencies. We establish new guarantees for KCPD under $m$-dependent data: specifically, we prove consistency in the number of detected change points and weak consistency in their locations under mild additional assumptions. We perform an LLM-based simulation that generates synthetic $m$-dependent text to validate the asymptotics. To complement these results, we present the first comprehensive empirical study of KCPD for text segmentation with modern embeddings. Across diverse text datasets, KCPD with text embeddings outperforms baselines in standard text segmentation metrics. We demonstrate through a case study on Taylor Swift's tweets that KCPD not only provides strong theoretical and simulated reliability but also practical effectiveness for text segmentation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper is withdrawn due to an error in the proof of Proposition 3, which is used to support Theorem 1"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03437","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:53Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:35:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04633","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04633","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04633","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Gienapp","name":"Gienapp, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Potthast","name":"Potthast, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Yates","name":"Yates, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harrisen","familyName":"Scells","name":"Scells, Harrisen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eugene","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Eugene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Topic-Specific Classifiers are Better Relevance Judges than Prompted LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:38:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T14:11:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:39:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The unjudged document problem, where systems that did not contribute to the original judgement pool may retrieve documents without a relevance judgement, is a key obstacle to the reuseability of test collections in information retrieval. While the de facto standard to deal with the problem is to treat unjudged documents as non-relevant, many alternatives have been proposed, such as the use of large language models (LLMs) as a relevance judge (LLM-as-a-judge). However, this has been criticized, among other things, as circular, since the same LLM can be used as the ranker and the judge. We propose to train topic-specific relevance classifiers instead: By finetuning monoT5 with independent LoRA weight adaptation on the judgments of a single assessor for a single topic's pool, we align it to that assessor's notion of relevance for the topic. The system rankings obtained through our classifier's relevance judgments achieve a Spearmans' $ρ$ correlation of $\u0026gt;0.94$ with ground truth system rankings. As little as 128 initial human judgments per topic suffice to improve the comparability of models, compared to treating unjudged documents as non-relevant, while achieving more reliability than existing LLM-as-a-judge approaches. Topic-specific relevance classifiers are thus a lightweight and straightforward way to tackle the unjudged document problem, while maintaining human judgments as the gold standard for retrieval evaluation. Code, models, and data are made openly available."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 3 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04633","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-26T02:49:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03869","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03869","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03869","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runhao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Runhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ziming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangzhen","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Guangzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring the Challenge and Value of Deep Learning in Automated Skin Disease Diagnosis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T16:37:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:38:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-16T14:43:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-19T01:39:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Skin cancer is one of the most prevalent and deadly forms of cancer worldwide, highlighting the critical importance of early detection and diagnosis in improving patient outcomes. Deep learning (DL) has shown significant promise in enhancing the accuracy and efficiency of automated skin disease diagnosis, particularly in detecting and classifying skin lesions. However, several challenges remain for DL-based skin cancer diagnosis, including complex features, image noise, intra-class variation, inter-class similarity, and data imbalance. This review synthesizes recent research and discusses innovative approaches to address these challenges, such as data augmentation, hybrid models, and feature fusion. Furthermore, the review highlights the integration of DL models into clinical workflows, offering insights into the potential of deep learning to revolutionize skin disease diagnosis and improve clinical decision-making. This review uniquely integrates a PRISMA-based methodology with a challenge-oriented taxonomy, providing a systematic and transparent synthesis of recent deep learning advances for skin disease diagnosis. It further highlights emerging directions such as hybrid CNN-Transformer architectures and uncertainty-aware models, emphasizing its contribution to future dermatological AI research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03869","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:00:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:00:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-19T02:45:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03687","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03687","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03687","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yanyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dexuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Dexuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenzhuo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Chenzhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihua","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Weihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedReflect: Teaching Medical LLMs to Self-Improve via Reflective Correction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T06:00:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:24:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-16T10:35:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-19T01:29:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical problem-solving demands expert knowledge and intricate reasoning. Recent studies of large language models (LLMs) attempt to ease this complexity by introducing external knowledge verification through retrieval-augmented generation or by training on reasoning datasets. However, these approaches suffer from drawbacks such as retrieval overhead and high annotation costs, and they heavily rely on substituted external assistants to reach limited performance in medical field. In this paper, we introduce MedReflect, a generalizable framework designed to inspire LLMs with a physician-like reflective thinking mode. MedReflect generates a single-pass reflection chain that includes initial hypothesis generation, self-questioning, self-answering and decision refinement. This self-verified and self-reflective nature releases large language model's latent capability in medical problem-solving without external retrieval or heavy annotation. We demonstrate that MedReflect enables cost-efficient medical dataset construction. With only a minimal subset of randomly sampled training examples and lightweight fine-tuning, this approach achieves notable absolute accuracy improvements across a series of medical benchmarks while significantly cutting annotation requirements. Our results provide evidence that LLMs can learn to solve specialized medical problems via self-reflection and self-improvement, reducing reliance on external supervision and extensive task-specific fine-tuning data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03687","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-19T02:45:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03731","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03731","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03731","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongfu","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Yongfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimizing Fine-Tuning through Advanced Initialization Strategies for Low-Rank Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T08:34:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:28:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-04T12:48:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T01:40:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T08:57:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:25:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-14T06:12:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:21:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid development of parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods has noticeably improved the efficiency of adapting large language models. Among these, LoRA has gained widespread popularity due to its strong balance of effectiveness and parameter efficiency. However, LoRA relies on initializing two low-rank matrices whose product is zero, which limits its ability to effectively activate and leverage the original model weights-creating a potential bottleneck for optimal performance. To address this limitation, we propose \\textbf{IniLoRA}, a novel initialization strategy that initializes the low-rank matrices to closely approximate the original model weights. Experimental results indicate that IniLoRA achieves better performance than LoRA across a range of models and tasks. Additionally, we introduce two variants, IniLoRA-$α$ and IniLoRA-$β$, both leveraging distinct initialization methods to enhance performance further."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03731","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:57:03Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:57:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:10:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03544","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03544","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03544","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuji","familyName":"Takubo","name":"Takubo, Yuji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniele","familyName":"Gammelli","name":"Gammelli, Daniele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Pavone","name":"Pavone, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"D'Amico","name":"D'Amico, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agile Tradespace Exploration for Space Rendezvous Mission Design via Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T22:28:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:13:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-11T22:27:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:57:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spacecraft rendezvous enables on-orbit servicing, debris removal, and crewed docking, forming the foundation for a scalable space economy. Designing such missions requires rapid exploration of the tradespace between control cost and flight time across multiple candidate targets. However, multi-objective optimization in this setting is challenging, as the underlying constraints are often nonconvex, and mission designers must balance accuracy (e.g., solving the full problem) with efficiency (e.g., convex relaxations), slowing iteration and limiting design agility. To address these challenges, this paper proposes an AI-powered framework that enables agile and generalized rendezvous mission design. Given the orbital information of the target spacecraft, boundary conditions of the servicer, and a range of flight times, a transformer model generates a set of near-Pareto optimal trajectories across varying flight times in a single parallelized inference step, thereby enabling rapid mission trade studies. The model is further extended to accommodate variable flight times and perturbed orbital dynamics, supporting realistic multi-objective trade-offs. Validation on chance-constrained rendezvous problems in Earth orbits with passive safety constraints demonstrates that the model generalizes across both flight times and dynamics, consistently providing high-quality initial guesses that converge to superior solutions in fewer iterations. Moreover, the framework efficiently approximates the Pareto front, achieving runtimes comparable to convex relaxation by exploiting parallelized inference. Together, these results position the proposed framework as a practical surrogate for nonconvex trajectory generation and mark an important step toward AI-driven trajectory design for accelerating preliminary mission planning in real-world rendezvous applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03544","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:26:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03405","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03405","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03405","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanket","familyName":"Badhe","name":"Badhe, Sanket","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LegalSim: Multi-Agent Simulation of Legal Systems for Discovering Procedural Exploits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:01:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:58:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2025.nllp-1.27","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present LegalSim, a modular multi-agent simulation of adversarial legal proceedings that explores how AI systems can exploit procedural weaknesses in codified rules. Plaintiff and defendant agents choose from a constrained action space (for example, discovery requests, motions, meet-and-confer, sanctions) governed by a JSON rules engine, while a stochastic judge model with calibrated grant rates, cost allocations, and sanction tendencies resolves outcomes. We compare four policies: PPO, a contextual bandit with an LLM, a direct LLM policy, and a hand-crafted heuristic; Instead of optimizing binary case outcomes, agents are trained and evaluated using effective win rate and a composite exploit score that combines opponent-cost inflation, calendar pressure, settlement pressure at low merit, and a rule-compliance margin. Across configurable regimes (e.g., bankruptcy stays, inter partes review, tax procedures) and heterogeneous judges, we observe emergent ``exploit chains'', such as cost-inflating discovery sequences and calendar-pressure tactics that remain procedurally valid yet systemically harmful. Evaluation via cross-play and Bradley-Terry ratings shows, PPO wins more often, the bandit is the most consistently competitive across opponents, the LLM trails them, and the heuristic is weakest. The results are stable in judge settings, and the simulation reveals emergent exploit chains, motivating red-teaming of legal rule systems in addition to model-level testing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages with 2 figures, accepted at the NLLP workshop at EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03405","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:26:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04141","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04141","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04141","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mayank","familyName":"Ravishankara","name":"Ravishankara, Mayank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varindra V. Persad","familyName":"Maharaj","name":"Maharaj, Varindra V. Persad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Artificial Intelligence Cognitive Examination: A Survey on the Evolution of Multimodal Evaluation from Recognition to Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:41:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-05T20:21:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-07T01:05:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/access.2025.3649182","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This survey paper chronicles the evolution of evaluation in multimodal artificial intelligence (AI), framing it as a progression of increasingly sophisticated \"cognitive examinations.\" We argue that the field is undergoing a paradigm shift, moving from simple recognition tasks that test \"what\" a model sees, to complex reasoning benchmarks that probe \"why\" and \"how\" it understands. This evolution is driven by the saturation of older benchmarks, where high performance often masks fundamental weaknesses. We chart the journey from the foundational \"knowledge tests\" of the ImageNet era to the \"applied logic and comprehension\" exams such as GQA and Visual Commonsense Reasoning (VCR), which were designed specifically to diagnose systemic flaws such as shortcut learning and failures in compositional generalization. We then survey the current frontier of \"expert-level integration\" benchmarks (e.g., MMBench, SEED-Bench, MMMU) designed for today's powerful multimodal large language models (MLLMs), which increasingly evaluate the reasoning process itself. Finally, we explore the uncharted territories of evaluating abstract, creative, and social intelligence. We conclude that the narrative of AI evaluation is not merely a history of datasets, but a continuous, adversarial process of designing better examinations that, in turn, redefine our goals for creating truly intelligent systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04141","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:13Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-07T02:59:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03663","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03663","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03663","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zeyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiming","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Caiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Sheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chien-Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UNIDOC-BENCH: A Unified Benchmark for Document-Centric Multimodal RAG"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T04:30:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:19:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T05:30:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:30:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-05T04:16:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-06T01:58:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal retrieval-augmented Generation (MM-RAG) is a key approach for applying large language models (LLMs) and agents to real-world knowledge bases, yet current evaluations are fragmented -- focusing on either text or images in isolation, or simplified multimodal setup, failing to capture document-centric multimodal use cases. In this paper, we introduce UniDoc-Bench, the first large-scale, realistic benchmark for MM-RAG built from $k$ real-world PDF pages across domains. Our pipeline extracts and links evidence from text, tables, and figures, then generates multimodal QA pairs spanning factual retrieval, comparison, summarization, and logical reasoning queries. To ensure reliability, all of QA pairs are validated by multiple human annotators and expert adjudication. UniDoc-Bench supports apples-to-apples comparison across four paradigms: 1) text-only, 2) image-only, 3) \\emph{multimodal} text-image fusion and 4) multimodal joint retrieval -- under a unified protocol with standardized candidate pools, prompts, and evaluation metrics. UniDoc-Bench can also be used to evaluate Visual Question Answering (VQA) tasks. Our experiments show that multimodal text-image fusion RAG systems consistently outperform both unimodal and jointly multimodal embedding-based retrieval, indicating that neither text nor images alone are sufficient and that current multimodal embeddings remain inadequate. Beyond benchmarking, our analysis reveals when and how visual context complements textual evidence, uncovers systematic failure modes, and offers actionable guidance for developing more robust MM-RAG pipelines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03663","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-06T04:10:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03289","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03289","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03289","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocheng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cynthia Xin","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Cynthia Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward Hong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Edward Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Why mask diffusion does not work"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T12:07:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-23T09:36:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-24T01:30:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The main advantages of diffusion language models over autoregressive (AR) models lie in their ability to support parallel generation and bidirectional attention, enabling a more controllable generation process. In recent years, open-source mask diffusion language models have emerged, most of which are based on a variant known as absorbing diffusion. However, this paper demonstrates why mask diffusion faces inherent difficulties in achieving parallel generation and bidirectional attention. We also propose the most effective training and inference strategies for mask diffusion."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03289","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:17Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-24T02:56:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrique","familyName":"Zuazua","name":"Zuazua, Enrique","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Machine Learning and Control: Foundations, Advances, and Perspectives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T10:47:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-17T21:25:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-19T01:10:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Control theory of dynamical systems offers a powerful framework for tackling challenges in deep neural networks and other machine learning architectures. We show that concepts such as simultaneous and ensemble controllability offer new insights into the classification and representation properties of deep neural networks, while the control and optimization of static systems can be employed to better understand the performance of shallow networks. Inspired by the classical concept of turnpike, we also explore the relationship between dynamic and static neural networks, where depth is traded for width, and the role of transformers as mechanisms for accelerating classical neural network tasks. We also exploit the expressive power of neural networks (exemplified, for instance, by the Universal Approximation Theorem) to develop a novel hybrid modeling methodology, the Hybrid-Cooperative Learning (HYCO), combining mechanics and data-driven methods in a game-theoretic setting. Finally, we describe how classical properties of diffusion processes, long established in the context of partial differential equations, contribute to explaining the success of modern generative artificial intelligence (AI). We present an overview of our recent results in these areas, illustrating how control, machine learning, numerical analysis, and partial differential equations come together to motivate a fertile ground for future research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:39Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-19T03:18:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04564","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04564","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04564","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Honglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunfan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yunfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Conditional Representation Learning for Customized Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:00:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-13T08:39:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T01:26:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conventional representation learning methods learn a universal representation that primarily captures dominant semantics, which may not always align with customized downstream tasks. For instance, in animal habitat analysis, researchers prioritize scene-related features, whereas universal embeddings emphasize categorical semantics, leading to suboptimal results. As a solution, existing approaches resort to supervised fine-tuning, which however incurs high computational and annotation costs. In this paper, we propose Conditional Representation Learning (CRL), aiming to extract representations tailored to arbitrary user-specified criteria. Specifically, we reveal that the semantics of a space are determined by its basis, thereby enabling a set of descriptive words to approximate the basis for a customized feature space. Building upon this insight, given a user-specified criterion, CRL first employs a large language model (LLM) to generate descriptive texts to construct the semantic basis, then projects the image representation into this conditional feature space leveraging a vision-language model (VLM). The conditional representation better captures semantics for the specific criterion, which could be utilized for multiple customized tasks. Extensive experiments on classification and retrieval tasks demonstrate the superiority and generality of the proposed CRL. The code is available at https://github.com/XLearning-SCU/2025-NeurIPS-CRL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04564","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:50:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04146","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04146","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04146","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minseo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Minseo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Coleman","familyName":"Hooper","name":"Hooper, Coleman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Tomar","name":"Tomar, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenfeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chenfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehrdad","familyName":"Farajtabar","name":"Farajtabar, Mehrdad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael W.","familyName":"Mahoney","name":"Mahoney, Michael W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kurt","familyName":"Keutzer","name":"Keutzer, Kurt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Gholami","name":"Gholami, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:50:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-15T16:36:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-16T02:44:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved state-of-the-art performance on a broad range of Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks, including document processing and code generation. Autoregressive Language Models (ARMs), which generate tokens sequentially conditioned on all previous tokens, have been the predominant paradigm for LLMs. While these models have achieved high accuracy across a range of downstream tasks, they exhibit low arithmetic intensity due to the inherent sequential dependency in next-token prediction. Recently, Diffusion Language Models (DLMs) have emerged as a promising alternative architecture. DLMs generate output tokens in parallel, mitigating the limitations of sequential decoding. However, the performance implications of DLMs relative to commonly deployed ARMs are not fully understood. In this work, we present a comprehensive study of the performance characteristics of ARMs and DLMs, combining theoretical analysis with empirical profiling to characterize the trade-offs between these approaches. We show that although DLMs can achieve higher arithmetic intensity than ARMs by leveraging parallelism across token positions, they fail to scale effectively with longer contexts. We then explore block-wise decoding for DLMs, which decouples arithmetic intensity from sequence length and enables better scaling to long contexts (similar to ARMs). We also examine batched inference and find that ARMs exhibit superior throughput as they benefit more from parallelism across sequences in the batch. Finally, we highlight opportunities for accelerating DLM inference, emphasizing that reducing the number of sampling steps is key for open-source DLMs to achieve lower latency relative to ARMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04146","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:50:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03291","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03291","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03291","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhuo","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yizhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanying","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Wanying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqi","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Wenqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanwei","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UniPruning: Unifying Local Metric and Global Feedback for Scalable Sparse LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T13:38:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-09T12:42:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-10T01:43:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) achieve strong performance across diverse tasks but face prohibitive computational and memory costs. Pruning offers a promising path by inducing sparsity while preserving architectural flexibility. However, existing methods struggle to balance efficiency and robustness: local metric approaches prune layer by layer but often collapse under high sparsity, whereas global feedback methods enforce consistency at the cost of expensive weight updates or restrictive semi-structured formats. We present UniPruning, a unified post-training pruning framework that combines the speed of local saliency metrics with the stability of global coordination, enabled by a mirror descent based optimization, all without updating model weights. UniPruning leverages fast layer-wise scoring and a lightweight global controller to allocate a single sparsity budget, supporting both unstructured and semi-structured N :M pruning within one framework. After a brief calibration, it can generate pruning masks for arbitrary sparsity levels in one shot, and adapts seamlessly to hardware-aware constraints. Extensive experiments on multiple pretrained LLM families and standard benchmarks show that UniPruning consistently delivers competitive or superior perplexity and zero-shot accuracy. Ablation studies further highlight the importance of mirror descent and local saliency anchoring. Overall, UniPruning provides an efficient, principled, and scalable solution for sparsifying large-scale LLMs. Our code is available at: https://github.com/RainbowQTT/UniPruning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03291","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-10T03:16:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04002","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04002","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04002","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunkui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yunkui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lanfei","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Lanfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nueraili","familyName":"Aierken","name":"Aierken, Nueraili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runhe","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Runhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjian","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hongjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yibin","familyName":"Ying","name":"Ying, Yibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgriGPT-VL: Agricultural Vision-Language Understanding Suite"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T02:30:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T13:17:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:47:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-07T17:46:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:57:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite rapid advances in multimodal large language models, agricultural applications remain constrained by the scarcity of domain-tailored models, curated vision-language corpora, and rigorous evaluation. To address these challenges, we present the AgriGPT-VL Suite, a unified multimodal framework for agriculture. Our contributions are threefold. First, we introduce Agri-3M-VL, the largest vision-language corpus for agriculture to our knowledge, curated by a scalable multi-agent data generator; it comprises 1M image-caption pairs, 2M image-grounded VQA pairs, 50K expert-level VQA instances, and 15K GRPO reinforcement learning samples. Second, we develop AgriGPT-VL, an agriculture-specialized vision-language model trained via a progressive curriculum of textual grounding, multimodal shallow/deep alignment, and GRPO refinement. This method achieves strong multimodal reasoning while preserving text-only capability. Third, we establish AgriBench-VL-4K, a compact yet challenging evaluation suite with open-ended and image-grounded questions, paired with multi-metric evaluation and an LLM-as-a-judge framework. Experiments show that AgriGPT-VL outperforms leading general-purpose VLMs on AgriBench-VL-4K, achieving higher pairwise win rates in the LLM-as-a-judge evaluation. Meanwhile, it remains competitive on the text-only AgriBench-13K with no noticeable degradation of language ability. Ablation studies further confirm consistent gains from our alignment and GRPO refinement stages. We will open source all of the resources to support reproducible research and deployment in low-resource agricultural settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04002","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-09T04:37:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04143","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04143","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04143","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantinos","familyName":"Kitsios","name":"Kitsios, Konstantinos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Sovrano","name":"Sovrano, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Earl T.","familyName":"Barr","name":"Barr, Earl T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"Bacchelli","name":"Bacchelli, Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detecting Semantic Clones of Unseen Functionality"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.13","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:45:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:23:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/ase63991.2025.00112","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semantic code clone detection is the task of detecting whether two snippets of code implement the same functionality (e.g., Sort Array). Recently, many neural models achieved near-perfect performance on this task. These models seek to make inferences based on their training data. Consequently, they better detect clones similar to those they have seen during training and may struggle to detect those they have not. Developers seeking clones are, of course, interested in both types of clones. We confirm this claim through a literature review, identifying three practical clone detection tasks in which the model's goal is to detect clones of a functionality even if it was trained on clones of different functionalities. In light of this finding, we re-evaluate six state-of-the-art models, including both task-specific models and generative LLMs, on the task of detecting clones of unseen functionality. Our experiments reveal a drop in F1 of up to 48% (average 31%) for task-specific models. LLMs perform on par with task-specific models without explicit training for clone detection, but generalize better to unseen functionalities, where F1 drops up to 5% (average 3%) instead. We propose and evaluate the use of contrastive learning to improve the performance of existing models on clones of unseen functionality. We draw inspiration from the computer vision and natural language processing fields where contrastive learning excels at measuring similarity between two objects, even if they come from classes unseen during training. We replace the final classifier of the task-specific models with a contrastive classifier, while for the generative LLMs we propose contrastive in-context learning, guiding the LLMs to focus on the differences between clones and non-clones. The F1 on clones of unseen functionality is improved by up to 26% (average 9%) for task-specific models and up to 5% (average 3%) for LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 3 figures, accepted for publication (to appear) in the 40th IEEE/ACM International Conference on Automated Software Engineering, ASE 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04143","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:16Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:47:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03805","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03805","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03805","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Canhui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Canhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiong","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Qiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenfang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwei","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xi","name":"Xi, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Token Length: Step Pruner for Efficient and Accurate Reasoning in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T13:24:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:34:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-22T13:19:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T01:32:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-29T03:47:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:12:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) demonstrate strong performance on complex tasks but often suffer from excessive verbosity, known as \"overthinking.\" Existing solutions via reinforcement learning (RL) typically penalize generated tokens to promote conciseness. However, these methods encounter two challenges: responses with fewer tokens do not always correspond to fewer reasoning steps, and models may develop hacking behavior in later stages of training by discarding reasoning steps to minimize token usage. In this work, we introduce \\textbf{Step Pruner (SP)}, an RL framework that steers LRMs toward more efficient reasoning by favoring compact reasoning steps. Our step-aware reward function prioritizes correctness while imposing penalties for redundant steps, and withholds rewards for incorrect responses to prevent the reinforcement of erroneous reasoning. Moreover, we propose a dynamic stopping mechanism: when the model's output no longer shortens, training is halted to prevent hacking behavior caused by the merging of steps. Extensive experiments across four reasoning benchmarks demonstrate that SP achieves state-of-the-art accuracy while significantly reducing response length. For instance, on AIME24, SP reduces token usage by \\textbf{69.7\\%}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03805","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:58:52Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:58:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:47:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03480","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03480","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03480","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vali","familyName":"Tawosi","name":"Tawosi, Vali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salwa","familyName":"Alamir","name":"Alamir, Salwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaomo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuela","familyName":"Veloso","name":"Veloso, Manuela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM Agents for Automated Dependency Upgrades"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T19:57:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:08:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-24T17:56:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T02:54:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As a codebase expands over time, its library dependencies can become outdated and require updates to maintain innovation and security. However, updating a library can introduce breaking changes in the code, necessitating significant developer time for maintenance. To address this, we introduce a framework of LLM agents to be used in combination with migration documentation to automatically recommend and apply code updates and ensure compatibility with new versions. Our solution can automatically localize updated library usages in live Java codebases and implement recommended fixes in a user-friendly manner. The system architecture consists of multiple key components: a Summary Agent, Control Agent, and Code Agent. To validate our approach, we apply the framework on an industrial use case by which we create three synthetic code repositories with major Upgrade changes and benchmark our approach against state-of-the-art methods. Results show that our approach not only performs upgrades using fewer tokens across all cases but also achieves a precision of 71.4%, highlighting its efficiency and effectiveness compared to state-of-the-art methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to AISM Workshop at ASE 2005"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03480","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:50:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:50:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:50:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03469","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03469","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03469","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keshav","familyName":"Ramani","name":"Ramani, Keshav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vali","familyName":"Tawosi","name":"Tawosi, Vali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salwa","familyName":"Alamir","name":"Alamir, Salwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Borrajo","name":"Borrajo, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging LLM Planning Agents and Formal Methods: A Case Study in Plan Verification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T19:46:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:08:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-24T18:17:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T02:55:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce a novel framework for evaluating the alignment between natural language plans and their expected behavior by converting them into Kripke structures and Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) using Large Language Models (LLMs) and performing model checking. We systematically evaluate this framework on a simplified version of the PlanBench plan verification dataset and report on metrics like Accuracy, Precision, Recall and F1 scores. Our experiments demonstrate that GPT-5 achieves excellent classification performance (F1 score of 96.3%) while almost always producing syntactically perfect formal representations that can act as guarantees. However, the synthesis of semantically perfect formal models remains an area for future exploration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to AgenticSE Workshop at ASE 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03469","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:50:39Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:50:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:50:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03463","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03463","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03463","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vali","familyName":"Tawosi","name":"Tawosi, Vali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keshav","familyName":"Ramani","name":"Ramani, Keshav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salwa","familyName":"Alamir","name":"Alamir, Salwa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaomo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ALMAS: an Autonomous LLM-based Multi-Agent Software Engineering Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T19:35:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:08:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-24T18:11:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T02:54:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent Large Language Model (LLM) systems have been leading the way in applied LLM research across a number of fields. One notable area is software development, where researchers have advanced the automation of code implementation, code testing, code maintenance, inter alia, using LLM agents. However, software development is a multifaceted environment that extends beyond just code. As such, a successful LLM system must factor in multiple stages of the software development life-cycle (SDLC). In this paper, we propose a vision for ALMAS, an Autonomous LLM-based Multi-Agent Software Engineering framework, which follows the above SDLC philosophy such that it may work within an agile software development team to perform several tasks end-to-end. ALMAS aligns its agents with agile roles, and can be used in a modular fashion to seamlessly integrate with human developers and their development environment. We showcase the progress towards ALMAS through our published works and a use case demonstrating the framework, where ALMAS is able to seamlessly generate an application and add a new feature."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to MAS-GAIN Workshop at ASE 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03463","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:50:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:50:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:50:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04325","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04325","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04325","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenechukwu","familyName":"Ogbuagu","name":"Ogbuagu, Kenechukwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sepehr","familyName":"Maleki","name":"Maleki, Sepehr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Bruni","name":"Bruni, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Senthil","familyName":"Krishnababu","name":"Krishnababu, Senthil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FoilDiff: A Hybrid Transformer Backbone for Diffusion-based Modelling of 2D Airfoil Flow Fields"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T19:10:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:04:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-18T12:45:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:47:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The accurate prediction of flow fields around airfoils is crucial for aerodynamic design and optimisation. Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) models are effective but computationally expensive, thus inspiring the development of surrogate models to enable quicker predictions. These surrogate models can be based on deep learning architectures, such as Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), and Diffusion Models (DMs). Diffusion models have shown significant promise in predicting complex flow fields. In this work, we propose FoilDiff, a diffusion-based surrogate model with a hybrid-backbone denoising network. This hybrid design combines the power of convolutional feature extraction and transformer-based global attention to generate more adaptable and accurate representations of flow structures. FoilDiff takes advantage of Denoising Diffusion Implicit Model (DDIM) sampling to optimise the efficiency of the sampling process at no additional cost to model generalisation. We used encoded representations of Reynolds number, angle of attack, and airfoil geometry to define the input space for generalisation across a wide range of aerodynamic conditions. When evaluated against state-of-the-art models, FoilDiff shows significant performance improvements, with mean prediction errors reducing by up to 85\\% on the same datasets. The results have demonstrated that FoilDiff can provide both more accurate predictions and better-calibrated predictive uncertainty than existing diffusion-based models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04325","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-19T03:17:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04108","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04108","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04108","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramzi","familyName":"Dakhmouche","name":"Dakhmouche, Ramzi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrien","familyName":"Letellier","name":"Letellier, Adrien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hossein","familyName":"Gorji","name":"Gorji, Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Linear Probes Measure LLM Uncertainty?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T09:14:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:52:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-17T13:43:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:50:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective Uncertainty Quantification (UQ) represents a key aspect for reliable deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) in automated decision-making and beyond. Yet, for LLM generation with multiple choice structure, the state-of-the-art in UQ is still dominated by the naive baseline given by the maximum softmax score. To address this shortcoming, we demonstrate that taking a principled approach via Bayesian statistics leads to improved performance despite leveraging the simplest possible model, namely linear regression. More precisely, we propose to train multiple Bayesian linear models, each predicting the output of a layer given the output of the previous one. Based on the obtained layer-level posterior distributions, we infer the global uncertainty level of the LLM by identifying a sparse combination of distributional features, leading to an efficient UQ scheme. Numerical experiments on various LLMs show consistent improvement over state-of-the-art baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04108","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:25Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:15:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03898","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03898","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03898","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nusrat Jahan","familyName":"Lia","name":"Lia, Nusrat Jahan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubhashis Roy","familyName":"Dipta","name":"Dipta, Shubhashis Roy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdullah Khan","familyName":"Zehady","name":"Zehady, Abdullah Khan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naymul","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Naymul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madhusodan","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Madhusodan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdullah Al","familyName":"Wasif","name":"Wasif, Abdullah Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Read Between the Lines: A Benchmark for Uncovering Political Bias in Bangla News Articles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T18:34:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-17T18:39:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T03:07:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Detecting media bias is crucial, specifically in the South Asian region. Despite this, annotated datasets and computational studies for Bangla political bias research remain scarce. Crucially because, political stance detection in Bangla news requires understanding of linguistic cues, cultural context, subtle biases, rhetorical strategies, code-switching, implicit sentiment, and socio-political background. To address this, we introduce the first benchmark dataset of 200 politically significant and highly debated Bangla news articles, labeled for government-leaning, government-critique, and neutral stances, alongside diagnostic analyses for evaluating large language models (LLMs). Our comprehensive evaluation of 28 proprietary and open-source LLMs shows strong performance in detecting government-critique content (F1 up to 0.83) but substantial difficulty with neutral articles (F1 as low as 0.00). Models also tend to over-predict government-leaning stances, often misinterpreting ambiguous narratives. This dataset and its associated diagnostics provide a foundation for advancing stance detection in Bangla media research and offer insights for improving LLM performance in low-resource languages."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to BLP at AACL-IJCNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03898","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:14Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:15:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03553","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03553","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03553","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hasibur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Hasibur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanan","familyName":"Salam","name":"Salam, Hanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CCD-Bench: Probing Cultural Conflict in Large Language Model Decision-Making"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T22:55:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:13:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-13T15:56:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-14T01:51:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although large language models (LLMs) are increasingly implicated in interpersonal and societal decision-making, their ability to navigate explicit conflicts between legitimately different cultural value systems remains largely unexamined. Existing benchmarks predominantly target cultural knowledge (CulturalBench), value prediction (WorldValuesBench), or single-axis bias diagnostics (CDEval); none evaluate how LLMs adjudicate when multiple culturally grounded values directly clash. We address this gap with CCD-Bench, a benchmark that assesses LLM decision-making under cross-cultural value conflict. CCD-Bench comprises 2,182 open-ended dilemmas spanning seven domains, each paired with ten anonymized response options corresponding to the ten GLOBE cultural clusters. These dilemmas are presented using a stratified Latin square to mitigate ordering effects. We evaluate 17 non-reasoning LLMs. Models disproportionately prefer Nordic Europe (mean 20.2 percent) and Germanic Europe (12.4 percent), while options for Eastern Europe and the Middle East and North Africa are underrepresented (5.6 to 5.8 percent). Although 87.9 percent of rationales reference multiple GLOBE dimensions, this pluralism is superficial: models recombine Future Orientation and Performance Orientation, and rarely ground choices in Assertiveness or Gender Egalitarianism (both under 3 percent). Ordering effects are negligible (Cramer's V less than 0.10), and symmetrized KL divergence shows clustering by developer lineage rather than geography. These patterns suggest that current alignment pipelines promote a consensus-oriented worldview that underserves scenarios demanding power negotiation, rights-based reasoning, or gender-aware analysis. CCD-Bench shifts evaluation beyond isolated bias detection toward pluralistic decision making and highlights the need for alignment strategies that substantively engage diverse worldviews."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03553","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-14T03:10:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03528","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03528","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03528","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Alajrami","name":"Alajrami, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingwei","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Xingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolaos","familyName":"Aletras","name":"Aletras, Nikolaos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine-Tuning on Noisy Instructions: Effects on Generalization and Performance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T21:54:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:12:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-11T22:25:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:09:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Instruction-tuning plays a vital role in enhancing the task-solving abilities of large language models (LLMs), improving their usability in generating helpful responses on various tasks. However, previous work has demonstrated that they are sensitive to minor variations in instruction phrasing. In this paper, we explore whether introducing perturbations in instruction-tuning data can enhance LLMs' resistance against noisy instructions. We focus on how instruction-tuning with perturbations, such as removing stop words or shuffling words, affects LLMs' performance on the original and perturbed versions of widely-used benchmarks (MMLU, BBH, GSM8K). We further assess learning dynamics and potential shifts in model behavior. Surprisingly, our results suggest that instruction-tuning on perturbed instructions can, in some cases, improve downstream performance. These findings highlight the importance of including perturbed instructions in instruction-tuning, which can make LLMs more resilient to noisy user inputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to AACL 2025 main conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03528","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-13T03:09:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04173","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04173","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04173","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soufiane","familyName":"Amini","name":"Amini, Soufiane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yassine","familyName":"Benajiba","name":"Benajiba, Yassine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cesare","familyName":"Bernardis","name":"Bernardis, Cesare","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Cayet","name":"Cayet, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassan","familyName":"Chafi","name":"Chafi, Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abderrahim","familyName":"Fathan","name":"Fathan, Abderrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Louis","familyName":"Faucon","name":"Faucon, Louis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Damien","familyName":"Hilloulin","name":"Hilloulin, Damien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungpack","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Sungpack","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ingo","familyName":"Kossyk","name":"Kossyk, Ingo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tran Minh Son","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Tran Minh Son","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rhicheek","familyName":"Patra","name":"Patra, Rhicheek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujith","familyName":"Ravi","name":"Ravi, Sujith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Schweizer","name":"Schweizer, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jyotika","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Jyotika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shailender","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Shailender","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Weiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kartik","familyName":"Talamadupula","name":"Talamadupula, Kartik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerry","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jerry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Open Agent Specification (Agent Spec): A Unified Representation for AI Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T12:26:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:56:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T09:29:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:36:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T11:55:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:32:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-07T14:02:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-10T01:41:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of agent frameworks has led to fragmentation in how agents are defined, executed, and evaluated. Existing systems differ in their abstractions, data flow semantics, and tool integrations, making it difficult to share or reproduce workflows. We introduce Open Agent Specification (Agent Spec), a declarative language that defines AI agents and agentic workflows in a way that is compatible across frameworks, promoting reusability, portability and interoperability of AI agents. Agent Spec defines a common set of components, control and data flow semantics, and schemas that allow an agent to be defined once and executed across different runtimes. Agent Spec also introduces a standardized Evaluation harness to assess agent behavior and agentic workflows across runtimes - analogous to how HELM and related harnesses standardized LLM evaluation - so that performance, robustness, and efficiency can be compared consistently across frameworks. We demonstrate this using four distinct runtimes (LangGraph, CrewAI, AutoGen, and WayFlow) evaluated over three different benchmarks (SimpleQA Verified, $τ^2$-Bench and BIRD-SQL). We provide accompanying toolsets: a Python SDK (PyAgentSpec), a reference runtime (WayFlow), and adapters for popular frameworks (e.g., LangGraph, AutoGen, CrewAI). Agent Spec bridges the gap between model-centric and agent-centric standardization \u0026amp; evaluation, laying the groundwork for reliable, reusable, and portable agentic systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04173","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:01Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-10T02:49:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03280","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03280","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03280","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinjie","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Jinjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longxu","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Longxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zili","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael Qizhe","familyName":"Shieh","name":"Shieh, Michael Qizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training Optimal Large Diffusion Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T16:20:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-05T08:32:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:30:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Quokka, the first systematic scaling law for diffusion language models (DLMs), encompassing both compute-constrained and data-constrained regimes, and studying the key modeling and optimization designs. Quokka is a good friend of Chinchilla and provides wider scopes. We hope the results would bring short-term practical guidance in DLMs training and long-term inspirations for the whole AI community."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03280","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-06T03:15:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04340","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04340","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04340","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anders","familyName":"Woodruff","name":"Woodruff, Anders","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niels","familyName":"Warncke","name":"Warncke, Niels","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arun","familyName":"Jose","name":"Jose, Arun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maxime","familyName":"Riché","name":"Riché, Maxime","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Demitri","familyName":"Africa","name":"Africa, David Demitri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mia","familyName":"Taylor","name":"Taylor, Mia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inoculation Prompting: Eliciting traits from LLMs during training can suppress them at test-time"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:04:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:05:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T03:52:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:22:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T08:49:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-17T00:37:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-03T12:21:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:33:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language model finetuning often results in learning undesirable traits in combination with desired ones. To address this, we propose inoculation prompting: modifying finetuning data by prepending a short system-prompt instruction that deliberately elicits the undesirable trait. At test time, we evaluate without the instruction; inoculated models have much lower expression of the trait than models trained with unmodified training data. Inoculation is selective: in a toy setting where assistant responses are always in Spanish and ALL-CAPS, an appropriate inoculation (e.g., ``You always speak in Spanish.'') teaches the model to capitalize responses while still responding in English. We find that inoculation is also effective across several additional settings: reducing emergent misalignment (EM) from task-specific finetuning, defending against backdoor injections, and mitigating the transmission of traits via subliminal learning. Follow-up analysis suggests a mechanism: making a trait less surprising via inoculation reduces optimization pressure to globally update the model, thereby reducing the degree of generalization. Our analysis relates to prior work on EM: inoculation explains prior findings that educational contexts mitigate EM from insecure code. Beyond demonstrating a simple and effective technique for selective learning, our results contribute to a better conceptual understanding of how and why language models generalize."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"40 pages, 22 figures. Under review at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04340","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-04T04:57:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03865","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03865","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03865","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenglei","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Chenglei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tailin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Tailin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unlocking Reasoning Capabilities in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning Exploration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T16:22:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:38:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-31T06:08:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:28:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) has recently enhanced the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs), particularly for mathematical problem solving. However, a fundamental limitation remains: as the sampling budget increases, the advantage of RLVR-trained models over their pretrained bases often diminishes or even vanishes, revealing a strong dependence on the base model's restricted search space. We attribute this phenomenon to the widespread use of the reverse Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence regularizer, whose mode-seeking behavior keeps the policy trapped inside the base model's support region and hampers wider exploration. To address this issue, we propose RAPO (Rewards-Aware Policy Optimization), an algorithm to promote broader yet focused exploration. Our method (i) utilizes the forward KL penalty to replace the reverse KL penalty for out-of-distribution exploration, and (ii) reweights the reference policy to facilitate adaptive in-distribution exploration. We train Qwen2.5-3B and 7B models with RAPO on the 8K SimpleRL-Zero dataset, without supervised fine-tuning, and evaluate them on AIME2024 and AIME2025. Results show that RAPO consistently improves problem-solving performance. Notably, RAPO enables models to surpass the base model's performance ceiling and solves previously intractable problems, advancing the frontier of RLVR for challenging reasoning tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03865","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:00:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:00:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-03T03:01:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03786","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03786","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03786","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T-Mai","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, T-Mai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fares","familyName":"Bougourzi","name":"Bougourzi, Fares","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fadi","familyName":"Dornaika","name":"Dornaika, Fadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinh Truong","familyName":"Hoang","name":"Hoang, Vinh Truong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MambaCAFU: Hybrid Multi-Scale and Multi-Attention Model with Mamba-Based Fusion for Medical Image Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T11:25:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:32:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T23:43:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:16:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, deep learning has shown near-expert performance in segmenting complex medical tissues and tumors. However, existing models are often task-specific, with performance varying across modalities and anatomical regions. Balancing model complexity and performance remains challenging, particularly in clinical settings where both accuracy and efficiency are critical. To address these issues, we propose a hybrid segmentation architecture featuring a three-branch encoder that integrates CNNs, Transformers, and a Mamba-based Attention Fusion (MAF) mechanism to capture local, global, and long-range dependencies. A multi-scale attention-based CNN decoder reconstructs fine-grained segmentation maps while preserving contextual consistency. Additionally, a co-attention gate enhances feature selection by emphasizing relevant spatial and semantic information across scales during both encoding and decoding, improving feature interaction and cross-scale communication. Extensive experiments on multiple benchmark datasets show that our approach outperforms state-of-the-art methods in accuracy and generalization, while maintaining comparable computational complexity. By effectively balancing efficiency and effectiveness, our architecture offers a practical and scalable solution for diverse medical imaging tasks. Source code and trained models will be publicly released upon acceptance to support reproducibility and further research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03786","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:58:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:58:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:18:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03490","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03490","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03490","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aneesha","familyName":"Sampath","name":"Sampath, Aneesha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oya","familyName":"Aran","name":"Aran, Oya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily Mower","familyName":"Provost","name":"Provost, Emily Mower","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SEER: The Span-based Emotion Evidence Retrieval Benchmark"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T20:15:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:09:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:07:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:20:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce the SEER (Span-based Emotion Evidence Retrieval) Benchmark to test Large Language Models' (LLMs) ability to identify the specific spans of text that express emotion. Unlike traditional emotion recognition tasks that assign a single label to an entire sentence, SEER targets the underexplored task of emotion evidence detection: pinpointing which exact phrases convey emotion. This span-level approach is crucial for applications like empathetic dialogue and clinical support, which need to know how emotion is expressed, not just what the emotion is. SEER includes two tasks: identifying emotion evidence within a single sentence, and identifying evidence across a short passage of five consecutive sentences. It contains new annotations for both emotion and emotion evidence on 1200 real-world sentences. We evaluate 14 open-source LLMs and find that, while some models approach average human performance on single-sentence inputs, their accuracy degrades in longer passages. Our error analysis reveals key failure modes, including overreliance on emotion keywords and false positives in neutral text."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03490","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:51:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:51:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-29T02:23:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03369","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03369","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03369","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huazhen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huazhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huimin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Huimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hainbin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hainbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lili","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangliang","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Liangliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenwen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wenwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TriQuest:An AI Copilot-Powered Platform for Interdisciplinary Curriculum Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T06:04:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:15:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T11:54:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:46:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Interdisciplinary teaching is a cornerstone of modern curriculum reform, but its implementation is hindered by challenges in knowledge integration and time-consuming lesson planning. Existing tools often lack the required pedagogical and domain-specific depth.We introduce TriQuest, an AI-copilot platform designed to solve these problems. TriQuest uses large language models and knowledge graphs via an intuitive GUI to help teachers efficiently generate high-quality interdisciplinary lesson plans. Its core features include intelligent knowledge integration from various disciplines and a human-computer collaborative review process to ensure quality and innovation.In a study with 43 teachers, TriQuest increased curriculum design efficiency and improved lesson plan quality. It also significantly lowered design barriers and cognitive load. Our work presents a new paradigm for empowering teacher professional development with intelligent technologies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03369","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:14Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-24T02:15:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03502","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03502","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03502","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Khairallah","name":"Khairallah, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arkaitz","familyName":"Zubiaga","name":"Zubiaga, Arkaitz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ALHD: A Large-Scale and Multigenre Benchmark Dataset for Arabic LLM-Generated Text Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T20:27:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:09:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-21T20:12:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:07:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce ALHD, the first large-scale comprehensive Arabic dataset explicitly designed to distinguish between human- and LLM-generated texts. ALHD spans three genres (news, social media, reviews), covering both MSA and dialectal Arabic, and contains over 400K balanced samples generated by three leading LLMs and originated from multiple human sources, which enables studying generalizability in Arabic LLM-genearted text detection. We provide rigorous preprocessing, rich annotations, and standardized balanced splits to support reproducibility. In addition, we present, analyze and discuss benchmark experiments using our new dataset, in turn identifying gaps and proposing future research directions. Benchmarking across traditional classifiers, BERT-based models, and LLMs (zero-shot and few-shot) demonstrates that fine-tuned BERT models achieve competitive performance, outperforming LLM-based models. Results are however not always consistent, as we observe challenges when generalizing across genres; indeed, models struggle to generalize when they need to deal with unseen patterns in cross-genre settings, and these challenges are particularly prominent when dealing with news articles, where LLM-generated texts resemble human texts in style, which opens up avenues for future research. ALHD establishes a foundation for research related to Arabic LLM-detection and mitigating risks of misinformation, academic dishonesty, and cyber threats."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"47 pages, 15 figures. Dataset available at Zenodo: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17249602 Codebase available at GitHub: https://github.com/alikhairallah/ALHD-Benchmarking"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03502","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:51:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:51:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-23T02:16:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03417","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03417","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03417","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javad Rafiei","familyName":"Asl","name":"Asl, Javad Rafiei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sidhant","familyName":"Narula","name":"Narula, Sidhant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Ghasemigol","name":"Ghasemigol, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduardo","familyName":"Blanco","name":"Blanco, Eduardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Takabi","name":"Takabi, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NEXUS: Network Exploration for eXploiting Unsafe Sequences in Multi-Turn LLM Jailbreaks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:24:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:50:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-21T17:41:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-22T01:06:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized natural language processing but remain vulnerable to jailbreak attacks, especially multi-turn jailbreaks that distribute malicious intent across benign exchanges and bypass alignment mechanisms. Existing approaches often explore the adversarial space poorly, rely on hand-crafted heuristics, or lack systematic query refinement. We present NEXUS (Network Exploration for eXploiting Unsafe Sequences), a modular framework for constructing, refining, and executing optimized multi-turn attacks. NEXUS comprises: (1) ThoughtNet, which hierarchically expands a harmful intent into a structured semantic network of topics, entities, and query chains; (2) a feedback-driven Simulator that iteratively refines and prunes these chains through attacker-victim-judge LLM collaboration using harmfulness and semantic-similarity benchmarks; and (3) a Network Traverser that adaptively navigates the refined query space for real-time attacks. This pipeline uncovers stealthy, high-success adversarial paths across LLMs. On several closed-source and open-source LLMs, NEXUS increases attack success rate by 2.1% to 19.4% over prior methods. Code: https://github.com/inspire-lab/NEXUS"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted in the main conference proceedings of the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP 2025). Javad Rafiei Asl and Sidhant Narula are co-first authors"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03417","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-22T02:21:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04774","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04774","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04774","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Weixu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Dorigo","name":"Dorigo, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mary Katherine","familyName":"Heinrich","name":"Heinrich, Mary Katherine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Online automatic code generation for robot swarms: LLMs and self-organizing hierarchy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:49:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:31:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-19T16:00:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:55:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Our recently introduced self-organizing nervous system (SoNS) provides robot swarms with 1) ease of behavior design and 2) global estimation of the swarm configuration and its collective environment, facilitating the implementation of online automatic code generation for robot swarms. In a demonstration with 6 real robots and simulation trials with \u0026gt;30 robots, we show that when a SoNS-enhanced robot swarm gets stuck, it can automatically solicit and run code generated by an external LLM on the fly, completing its mission with an 85% success rate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This abstract was accepted to and presented at the \"Multi-Agent Cooperative Systems and Swarm Robotics in the Era of Generative AI\" (MACRAI) workshop at the 2025 IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems (IROS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04774","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Om","familyName":"Tailor","name":"Tailor, Om","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Audit the Whisper: Detecting Steganographic Collusion in Multi-Agent LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T17:51:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:03:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-17T19:12:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:07:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent deployments of large language models (LLMs) are increasingly embedded in market, allocation, and governance workflows, yet covert coordination among agents can silently erode trust and social welfare. Existing audits are dominated by heuristics that lack theoretical guarantees, struggle to transfer across tasks, and seldom ship with the infrastructure needed for independent replication. We introduce Audit the Whisper, a conference-grade research artifact that spans theory, benchmark design, detection, and reproducibility. Our contributions are: (i) a channel-capacity analysis showing how interventions such as paraphrase, rate limiting, and role permutation impose quantifiable capacity penalties-operationalised via paired-run Kullback--Leibler diagnostics-that tighten mutual-information thresholds with finite-sample guarantees and full proofs; (ii) ColludeBench-v0, covering pricing, first-price auctions, peer review, and hosted Gemini/Groq APIs with configurable covert schemes, deterministic manifests, and reward instrumentation; and (iii) a calibrated auditing pipeline that fuses cross-run mutual information, permutation invariance, watermark variance, and fairness-aware acceptance bias, each tuned to a $10^{-3}$ false-positive budget and validated by 10k honest runs plus an e-value martingale. Across ColludeBench and external suites including Secret Collusion, CASE, Perfect Collusion Benchmark, and SentinelAgent, the union meta-test attains state-of-the-art power at fixed FPR while ablations surface price-of-auditing trade-offs and fairness-driven colluders invisible to MI alone. We release regeneration scripts, anonymized manifests, and documentation so that external auditors can reproduce every figure, satisfy double-blind requirements, and extend the framework with minimal effort."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 0 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04384","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04384","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04384","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Ballew","name":"Ballew, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingbo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaogang","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Shaogang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM Based Bayesian Optimization for Prompt Search"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T22:32:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:08:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T06:37:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T00:30:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bayesian Optimization (BO) has been widely used to efficiently optimize expensive black-box functions with limited evaluations. In this paper, we investigate the use of BO for prompt engineering to enhance text classification with Large Language Models (LLMs). We employ an LLM-powered Gaussian Process (GP) as the surrogate model to estimate the performance of different prompt candidates. These candidates are generated by an LLM through the expansion of a set of seed prompts and are subsequently evaluated using an Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) acquisition function in conjunction with the GP posterior. The optimization process iteratively refines the prompts based on a subset of the data, aiming to improve classification accuracy while reducing the number of API calls by leveraging the prediction uncertainty of the LLM-based GP. The proposed BO-LLM algorithm is evaluated on two datasets, and its advantages are discussed in detail in this paper."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04384","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:13:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-17T04:19:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03567","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03567","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03567","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fatmazohra","familyName":"Rezkellah","name":"Rezkellah, Fatmazohra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramzi","familyName":"Dakhmouche","name":"Dakhmouche, Ramzi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Machine Unlearning Meets Adversarial Robustness via Constrained Interventions on LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T23:32:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:14:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T07:57:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:33:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T16:42:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-17T01:05:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the increasing adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs), more customization is needed to ensure privacy-preserving and safe generation. We address this objective from two critical aspects: unlearning of sensitive information and robustness to jail-breaking attacks. We investigate various constrained optimization formulations that address both aspects in a \\emph{unified manner}, by finding the smallest possible interventions on LLM weights that either make a given vocabulary set unreachable or embed the LLM with robustness to tailored attacks by shifting part of the weights to a \\emph{safer} region. Beyond unifying two key properties, this approach contrasts with previous work in that it doesn't require an oracle classifier that is typically not available or represents a computational overhead. Surprisingly, we find that the simplest point-wise constraint-based intervention we propose leads to better performance than max-min interventions, while having a lower computational cost. Comparison against state-of-the-art defense methods demonstrates superior performance of the proposed approach."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03567","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:53:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:53:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-17T04:19:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03394","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03394","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03394","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghwan","familyName":"Rho","name":"Rho, Donghwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Studying the Korean Word-Chain Game with RLVR: Mitigating Reward Conflicts via Curriculum Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:00:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:04:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T04:07:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:24:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) is a promising approach for training large language models (LLMs) with stronger reasoning abilities. It has also been applied to a variety of logic puzzles. In this work, we study the Korean word-chain game using RLVR. We show that rule-derived rewards can naturally conflict, and demonstrate through experiments that a curriculum-learning scheme mitigates these conflicts. Our findings motivate further studies of puzzle tasks in diverse languages."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03394","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:26:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04587","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04587","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04587","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiming","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Ruiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charles","familyName":"Herndon","name":"Herndon, Charles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunsuke","familyName":"Koga","name":"Koga, Shunsuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeanne","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Jeanne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pathology-CoT: Learning Visual Chain-of-Thought Agent from Expert Whole Slide Image Diagnosis Behavior"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:44:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:20:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T18:37:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:03:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diagnosing a whole-slide image is an interactive, multi-stage process of changing magnification and moving between fields. Although recent pathology foundation models demonstrated superior performances, practical agentic systems that decide what field to examine next, adjust magnification, and deliver explainable diagnoses are still lacking. Such limitation is largely bottlenecked by data: scalable, clinically aligned supervision of expert viewing behavior that is tacit and experience-based, not documented in textbooks or internet, and therefore absent from LLM training. Here we introduce a framework designed to address this challenge through three key breakthroughs. First, the AI Session Recorder seamlessly integrates with standard whole-slide image viewers to unobtrusively record routine navigation and convert the viewer logs into standardized behavioral commands and bounding boxes. Second, a lightweight human-in-the-loop review turns AI-drafted rationales for behavioral commands into the Pathology-CoT dataset, a form of paired \"where to look\" and \"why it matters\", enabling six-fold faster labeling compared to manual constructing such Chain-of-Thought dataset. Using this behavioral data, we build Pathology-o3, a two-stage agent that first proposes important ROIs and then performs behavior-guided reasoning. On the gastrointestinal lymph-node metastasis detection task, our method achieved 100 recall on the internal validation from Stanford Medicine and 97.6 recall on an independent external validation from Sweden, exceeding the state-of-the-art OpenAI o3 model and generalizing across backbones. To our knowledge, Pathology-CoT constitutes one of the first behavior-grounded agentic systems in pathology. Turning everyday viewer logs into scalable, expert-validated supervision, our framework makes agentic pathology practical and establishes a path to human-aligned, upgradeable clinical AI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04587","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-15T02:22:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04716","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04716","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04716","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maximilian R. P.","familyName":"von Liechtenstein","name":"von Liechtenstein, Maximilian R. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Complexity (cs.CC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.1.1; F.2.2; I.2.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68Q17, 68Q25","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T11:34:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:28:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T11:54:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T02:31:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-11T07:06:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:26:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Curved Boolean Logic (CBL) generalizes propositional logic by allowing local truth assignments that do not extend to a single global valuation, analogous to curvature in geometry. We give equivalent sheaf and exclusivity-graph semantics and a context-aware proof calculus that is conservative in the flat limit. We formalize CBL-SAT and basic complexity (NP-complete in general) and present operational operators (CBL-AC and CBL-CONS) that prune contradictions earlier on classical hardware. We model noise with iid, AR(1)-correlated, and adversarial bounded perturbations and provide permutation-based significance with Benjamini-Hochberg FDR control. A Colab-ready notebook (ancillary files) regenerates all figures and statistics. We position CBL relative to KCBS, CSW, and sheaf frameworks and outline links to SAT/CSP and robustness/adapter stability in large language models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v3: Restores original v1 content; later additions are retracted pending a normalization audit"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04716","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:21:17Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:21:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04550","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04550","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04550","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenwei","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Zhenwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"He","name":"He, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianfeng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanqing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Hanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juanhui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juanhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayuan","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Jiayuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhabrata","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Subhabrata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suhang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Suhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiliang","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoit","familyName":"Dumoulin","name":"Dumoulin, Benoit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TRAJECT-Bench:A Trajectory-Aware Benchmark for Evaluating Agentic Tool Use"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:30:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:18:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T09:19:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:31:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM)-based agents increasingly rely on tool use to complete real-world tasks. While existing works evaluate the LLMs' tool use capability, they largely focus on the final answers yet overlook the detailed tool usage trajectory, i.e., whether tools are selected, parameterized, and ordered correctly. We introduce TRAJECT-Bench, a trajectory-aware benchmark to comprehensively evaluate LLMs' tool use capability through diverse tasks with fine-grained evaluation metrics. TRAJECT-Bench pairs high-fidelity, executable tools across practical domains with tasks grounded in production-style APIs, and synthesizes trajectories that vary in breadth (parallel calls) and depth (interdependent chains). Besides final accuracy, TRAJECT-Bench also reports trajectory-level diagnostics, including tool selection and argument correctness, and dependency/order satisfaction. Analyses reveal failure modes such as similar tool confusion and parameter-blind selection, and scaling behavior with tool diversity and trajectory length where the bottleneck of transiting from short to mid-length trajectories is revealed, offering actionable guidance for LLMs' tool use."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04550","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04479","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04479","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04479","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nonghai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Nonghai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiazi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VaseVQA-3D: Benchmarking 3D VLMs on Ancient Greek Pottery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:28:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:14:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T23:14:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:12:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have achieved significant progress in multimodal understanding tasks, demonstrating strong capabilities particularly in general tasks such as image captioning and visual reasoning. However, when dealing with specialized cultural heritage domains like 3D vase artifacts, existing models face severe data scarcity issues and insufficient domain knowledge limitations. Due to the lack of targeted training data, current VLMs struggle to effectively handle such culturally significant specialized tasks. To address these challenges, we propose the VaseVQA-3D dataset, which serves as the first 3D visual question answering dataset for ancient Greek pottery analysis, collecting 664 ancient Greek vase 3D models with corresponding question-answer data and establishing a complete data construction pipeline. We further develop the VaseVLM model, enhancing model performance in vase artifact analysis through domain-adaptive training. Experimental results validate the effectiveness of our approach, where we improve by 12.8% on R@1 metrics and by 6.6% on lexical similarity compared with previous state-of-the-art on the VaseVQA-3D dataset, significantly improving the recognition and understanding of 3D vase artifacts, providing new technical pathways for digital heritage preservation research. Code: https://github.com/AIGeeksGroup/VaseVQA-3D. Website: https://aigeeksgroup.github.io/VaseVQA-3D."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04479","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04064","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04064","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04064","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingxiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lujia","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Lujia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decoding Emotion in the Deep: A Systematic Study of How LLMs Represent, Retain, and Express Emotion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T06:53:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-12T17:53:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:11:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly expected to navigate the nuances of human emotion. While research confirms that LLMs can simulate emotional intelligence, their internal emotional mechanisms remain largely unexplored. This paper investigates the latent emotional representations within modern LLMs by asking: how, where, and for how long is emotion encoded in their neural architecture? To address this, we introduce a novel, large-scale Reddit corpus of approximately 400,000 utterances, balanced across seven basic emotions through a multi-stage process of classification, rewriting, and synthetic generation. Using this dataset, we employ lightweight \"probes\" to read out information from the hidden layers of various Qwen3 and LLaMA models without altering their parameters. Our findings reveal that LLMs develop a surprisingly well-defined internal geometry of emotion, which sharpens with model scale and significantly outperforms zero-shot prompting. We demonstrate that this emotional signal is not a final-layer phenomenon but emerges early and peaks mid-network. Furthermore, the internal states are both malleable (they can be influenced by simple system prompts) and persistent, as the initial emotional tone remains detectable for hundreds of subsequent tokens. We contribute our dataset, an open-source probing toolkit, and a detailed map of the emotional landscape within LLMs, offering crucial insights for developing more transparent and aligned AI systems. The code and dataset are open-sourced."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 7 figures, 4 tables. Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04064","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03418","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03418","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03418","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ananya","familyName":"Mantravadi","name":"Mantravadi, Ananya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shivali","familyName":"Dalmia","name":"Dalmia, Shivali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olga","familyName":"Pospelova","name":"Pospelova, Olga","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhishek","familyName":"Mukherji","name":"Mukherji, Abhishek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nand","familyName":"Dave","name":"Dave, Nand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anudha","familyName":"Mittal","name":"Mittal, Anudha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LegalWiz: A Multi-Agent Generation Framework for Contradiction Detection in Legal Documents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:24:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:05:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T19:46:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:07:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) integrates large language models (LLMs) with external sources, but unresolved contradictions in retrieved evidence often lead to hallucinations and legally unsound outputs. Benchmarks currently used for contradiction detection lack domain realism, cover only limited conflict types, and rarely extend beyond single-sentence pairs, making them unsuitable for legal applications. Controlled generation of documents with embedded contradictions is therefore essential: it enables systematic stress-testing of models, ensures coverage of diverse conflict categories, and provides a reliable basis for evaluating contradiction detection and resolution. We present a multi-agent contradiction-aware benchmark framework for the legal domain that generates synthetic legal-style documents, injects six structured contradiction types, and models both self- and pairwise inconsistencies. Automated contradiction mining is combined with human-in-the-loop validation to guarantee plausibility and fidelity. This benchmark offers one of the first structured resources for contradiction-aware evaluation in legal RAG pipelines, supporting more consistent, interpretable, and trustworthy systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03418","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:26Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04503","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04503","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04503","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqian","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Shiqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaobao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongliang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zhongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanhao","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Yanhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anh Tuan","familyName":"Luu","name":"Luu, Anh Tuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"P2P: A Poison-to-Poison Remedy for Reliable Backdoor Defense in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T05:45:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:15:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:31:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:15:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"During fine-tuning, large language models (LLMs) are increasingly vulnerable to data-poisoning backdoor attacks, which compromise their reliability and trustworthiness. However, existing defense strategies suffer from limited generalization: they only work on specific attack types or task settings. In this study, we propose Poison-to-Poison (P2P), a general and effective backdoor defense algorithm. P2P injects benign triggers with safe alternative labels into a subset of training samples and fine-tunes the model on this re-poisoned dataset by leveraging prompt-based learning. This enforces the model to associate trigger-induced representations with safe outputs, thereby overriding the effects of original malicious triggers. Thanks to this robust and generalizable trigger-based fine-tuning, P2P is effective across task settings and attack types. Theoretically and empirically, we show that P2P can neutralize malicious backdoors while preserving task performance. We conduct extensive experiments on classification, mathematical reasoning, and summary generation tasks, involving multiple state-of-the-art LLMs. The results demonstrate that our P2P algorithm significantly reduces the attack success rate compared with baseline models. We hope that the P2P can serve as a guideline for defending against backdoor attacks and foster the development of a secure and trustworthy LLM community."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04503","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:17:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03683","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03683","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03683","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nisar","familyName":"Hussain","name":"Hussain, Nisar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amna","familyName":"Qasim","name":"Qasim, Amna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gull","familyName":"Mehak","name":"Mehak, Gull","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad","familyName":"Zain","name":"Zain, Muhammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Momina","familyName":"Hafeez","name":"Hafeez, Momina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grigori","familyName":"Sidorov","name":"Sidorov, Grigori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine-Tuning Large Language Models with QLoRA for Offensive Language Detection in Roman Urdu-English Code-Mixed Text"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T05:38:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:24:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T05:42:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:24:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The use of derogatory terms in languages that employ code mixing, such as Roman Urdu, presents challenges for Natural Language Processing systems due to unstated grammar, inconsistent spelling, and a scarcity of labeled data. In this work, we propose a QLoRA based fine tuning framework to improve offensive language detection in Roman Urdu-English text. We translated the Roman Urdu-English code mixed dataset into English using Google Translate to leverage English LLMs, while acknowledging that this translation reduces direct engagement with code mixing features. Our focus is on classification performance using English translated low resource inputs. We fine tuned several transformers and large language models, including Meta LLaMA 3 8B, Mistral 7B v0.1, LLaMA 2 7B, ModernBERT, and RoBERTa, with QLoRA for memory efficient adaptation. Models were trained and evaluated on a manually annotated Roman Urdu dataset for offensive vs non offensive content. Of all tested models, the highest F1 score of 91.45 was attained by Meta LLaMA 3 8B, followed by Mistral 7B at 89.66, surpassing traditional transformer baselines. These results demonstrate the efficacy of QLoRA in fine tuning high performing models for low resource environments such as code mixed offensive language detection, and confirm the potential of LLMs for this task. This work advances a scalable approach to Roman Urdu moderation and paves the way for future multilingual offensive detection systems based on LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 22 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03683","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:51Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:17:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03298","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03298","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03298","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongqi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Dongqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjin","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Wenjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CAFL-L: Constraint-Aware Federated Learning with Lagrangian Dual Optimization for On-Device Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T22:07:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T15:06:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:55:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Constraint-Aware Federated Learning with Lagrangian Dual Optimization (CAFL-L), a principled extension of FedAvg that explicitly incorporates device-level resource constraints including energy, communication, memory, and thermal budgets. CAFL-L employs Lagrangian dual optimization to dynamically adapt training hyperparameters -- freezing depth, local steps, batch size, and communication compression -- while preserving training stability through token-budget preservation via gradient accumulation. Experiments on a character-level language model demonstrate that CAFL-L achieves superior constraint satisfaction compared to standard FedAvg (reducing memory usage by 20% and communication by 95%) while maintaining competitive validation performance, making it practical for deployment on resource-constrained edge devices."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by 39th NeurIPS - Constrained Optimization for Machine Learning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03298","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:32Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:17:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04363","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04363","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04363","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunjun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyunjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sejong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sejong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MacroBench: A Novel Testbed for Web Automation Scripts via Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:15:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:06:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T20:09:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:08:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce MacroBench, a code-first benchmark that evaluates whether LLMs can synthesize reusable browser-automation programs (macros) from natural-language goals by reading HTML/DOM and emitting Selenium. MacroBench instantiates seven self-hosted sites covering 681 tasks across interaction complexity and targeting difficulty. Our end-to-end protocol validates generated code via static checks, sandboxed execution, and outcome verification (DOM assertions, database snapshots), and includes a safety suite for scraping, spam/abuse, and credential/privacy prompts. Across 2,636 model-task runs, we observe stratified success: GPT-4o-mini (96.8%), GPT-4o (95.3%), Gemini (89.0%), DeepSeek (83.4%). Models handle simple tasks reliably (91.7%) but fail on complex workflows (0.0%), and none meet production-quality coding practices despite functional completion. We release our complete benchmark pipeline, evaluation framework, and experimental results at https://github.com/hyunjun1121/MacroBench to enable reproducible assessment of macro synthesis for web automation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Lock-LLM"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04363","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:40Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04097","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04097","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04097","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peichao","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Peichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhui","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Jinhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kexuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kexuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningchang","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Ningchang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yilei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WebRenderBench: Enhancing Web Interface Generation through Layout-Style Consistency and Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T08:47:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:52:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T03:04:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:24:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automating the conversion of UI images into web code is a critical task for front-end development and rapid prototyping. Advances in multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have made WebUI-to-Code increasingly feasible, yet existing benchmarks remain limited in data diversity and evaluation reliability. To address these issues, we present WebRenderBench, a large-scale benchmark of 45.1k webpages collected from real-world portal sites, offering greater diversity, complexity, and realism than prior benchmarks. We further propose a novel evaluation metric that measures layout and style consistency from the final rendered pages. Unlike vision-based methods that rely on costly LLM reasoning or structure-based comparisons vulnerable to noise and asymmetry, our approach enables more efficient, objective, and reliable UI quality assessment. Finally, we introduce the Automated Layout and Style Inspection Agent (ALISA), which integrates this metric into reinforcement learning as a reward signal to enhance training on crawled asymmetric webpages. Experiments show that ALISA significantly boosts generation performance, achieving state-of-the-art results across multiple metrics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04097","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:09Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03363","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03363","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03363","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxiu","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Mingxiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaochang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Gaochang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingqiao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lingqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dacheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Dacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyou","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Tianyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiatian","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xiatian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unified Unsupervised Anomaly Detection via Matching Cost Filtering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T03:28:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T12:00:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T02:32:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unsupervised anomaly detection (UAD) aims to identify image- and pixel-level anomalies using only normal training data, with wide applications such as industrial inspection and medical analysis, where anomalies are scarce due to privacy concerns and cold-start constraints. Existing methods, whether reconstruction-based (restoring normal counterparts) or embedding-based (pretrained representations), fundamentally conduct image- or feature-level matching to generate anomaly maps. Nonetheless, matching noise has been largely overlooked, limiting their detection ability. Beyond earlier focus on unimodal RGB-based UAD, recent advances expand to multimodal scenarios, e.g., RGB-3D and RGB-Text, enabled by point cloud sensing and vision-language models. Despite shared challenges, these lines remain largely isolated, hindering a comprehensive understanding and knowledge transfer. In this paper, we advocate unified UAD for both unimodal and multimodal settings in the matching perspective. Under this insight, we present Unified Cost Filtering (UCF), a generic post-hoc refinement framework for refining anomaly cost volume of any UAD model. The cost volume is constructed by matching a test sample against normal samples from the same or different modalities, followed by a learnable filtering module with multi-layer attention guidance from the test sample, mitigating matching noise and highlighting subtle anomalies. Comprehensive experiments on 22 diverse benchmarks demonstrate the efficacy of UCF in enhancing a variety of UAD methods, consistently achieving new state-of-the-art results in both unimodal (RGB) and multimodal (RGB-3D, RGB-Text) UAD scenarios. Code and models will be released at https://github.com/ZHE-SAPI/CostFilter-AD."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"63 pages (main paper and supplementary material), 39 figures, 58 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03363","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:05Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04764","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04764","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04764","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raha","familyName":"Askari","name":"Askari, Raha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sina","familyName":"Zarrieß","name":"Zarrieß, Sina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Özge","familyName":"Alacam","name":"Alacam, Özge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Judith","familyName":"Sieker","name":"Sieker, Judith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are BabyLMs Deaf to Gricean Maxims? A Pragmatic Evaluation of Sample-efficient Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:38:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:31:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T09:14:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:35:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Implicit meanings are integral to human communication, making it essential for language models to be capable of identifying and interpreting them. Grice (1975) proposed a set of conversational maxims that guide cooperative dialogue, noting that speakers may deliberately violate these principles to express meanings beyond literal words, and that listeners, in turn, recognize such violations to draw pragmatic inferences.\n Building on Surian et al. (1996)'s study of children's sensitivity to violations of Gricean maxims, we introduce a novel benchmark to test whether language models pretrained on less than 10M and less than 100M tokens can distinguish maxim-adhering from maxim-violating utterances. We compare these BabyLMs across five maxims and situate their performance relative to children and a Large Language Model (LLM) pretrained on 3T tokens.\n We find that overall, models trained on less than 100M tokens outperform those trained on less than 10M, yet fall short of child-level and LLM competence. Our results suggest that modest data increases improve some aspects of pragmatic behavior, leading to finer-grained differentiation between pragmatic dimensions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for the BabyLM workshop in EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04764","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:21:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04759","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04759","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04759","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Progressive Gaussian Transformer with Anisotropy-aware Sampling for Open Vocabulary Occupancy Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:36:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:30:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T09:34:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:36:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The 3D occupancy prediction task has witnessed remarkable progress in recent years, playing a crucial role in vision-based autonomous driving systems. While traditional methods are limited to fixed semantic categories, recent approaches have moved towards predicting text-aligned features to enable open-vocabulary text queries in real-world scenes. However, there exists a trade-off in text-aligned scene modeling: sparse Gaussian representation struggles to capture small objects in the scene, while dense representation incurs significant computational overhead. To address these limitations, we present PG-Occ, an innovative Progressive Gaussian Transformer Framework that enables open-vocabulary 3D occupancy prediction. Our framework employs progressive online densification, a feed-forward strategy that gradually enhances the 3D Gaussian representation to capture fine-grained scene details. By iteratively enhancing the representation, the framework achieves increasingly precise and detailed scene understanding. Another key contribution is the introduction of an anisotropy-aware sampling strategy with spatio-temporal fusion, which adaptively assigns receptive fields to Gaussians at different scales and stages, enabling more effective feature aggregation and richer scene information capture. Through extensive evaluations, we demonstrate that PG-Occ achieves state-of-the-art performance with a relative 14.3% mIoU improvement over the previous best performing method. Code and pretrained models will be released upon publication on our project page: https://yanchi-3dv.github.io/PG-Occ"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://yanchi-3dv.github.io/PG-Occ"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04759","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:21:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03470","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03470","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03470","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoit","familyName":"Dherin","name":"Dherin, Benoit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Munn","name":"Munn, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On residual network depth"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T19:48:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:08:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T23:37:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:13:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep residual architectures, such as ResNet and the Transformer, have enabled models of unprecedented depth, yet a formal understanding of why depth is so effective remains an open question. A popular intuition, following Veit et al. (2016), is that these residual networks behave like ensembles of many shallower models. Our key finding is an explicit analytical formula that verifies this ensemble perspective, proving that increasing network depth is mathematically equivalent to expanding the size of this implicit ensemble. Furthermore, our expansion reveals a hierarchical ensemble structure in which the combinatorial growth of computation paths leads to an explosion in the output signal, explaining the historical necessity of normalization layers in training deep models. This insight offers a first principles explanation for the historical dependence on normalization layers and sheds new light on a family of successful normalization-free techniques like SkipInit and Fixup. However, while these previous approaches infer scaling factors through optimizer analysis or a heuristic analogy to Batch Normalization, our work offers the first explanation derived directly from the network's inherent functional structure. Specifically, our Residual Expansion Theorem reveals that scaling each residual module provides a principled solution to taming the combinatorial explosion inherent to these architectures. We further show that this scaling acts as a capacity controls that also implicitly regularizes the model's complexity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03470","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:50:40Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:50:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:21:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04631","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04631","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04631","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anastasia","familyName":"Zhukova","name":"Zhukova, Anastasia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Lührs","name":"Lührs, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian E.","familyName":"Lobmüller","name":"Lobmüller, Christian E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bela","familyName":"Gipp","name":"Gipp, Bela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Contrastive Learning Using Graph Embeddings for Domain Adaptation of Language Models in the Process Industry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:36:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T12:23:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:44:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent trends in NLP utilize knowledge graphs (KGs) to enhance pretrained language models by incorporating additional knowledge from the graph structures to learn domain-specific terminology or relationships between documents that might otherwise be overlooked. This paper explores how SciNCL, a graph-aware neighborhood contrastive learning methodology originally designed for scientific publications, can be applied to the process industry domain, where text logs contain crucial information about daily operations and are often structured as sparse KGs. Our experiments demonstrate that language models fine-tuned with triplets derived from graph embeddings (GE) outperform a state-of-the-art mE5-large text encoder by 9.8-14.3% (5.45-7.96p) on the proprietary process industry text embedding benchmark (PITEB) while having 3 times fewer parameters."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted to EMNLP 2025 (industry track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04631","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:27:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04654","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04654","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04654","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andy","familyName":"Cǎtrunǎ","name":"Cǎtrunǎ, Andy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrian","familyName":"Cosma","name":"Cosma, Adrian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emilian","familyName":"Rǎdoi","name":"Rǎdoi, Emilian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoME: Estimating Psychological Traits from Gait with Multi-Stage Mixture of Movement Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:58:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:24:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T08:51:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:34:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Gait encodes rich biometric and behavioural information, yet leveraging the manner of walking to infer psychological traits remains a challenging and underexplored problem. We introduce a hierarchical Multi-Stage Mixture of Movement Experts (MoME) architecture for multi-task prediction of psychological attributes from gait sequences represented as 2D poses. MoME processes the walking cycle in four stages of movement complexity, employing lightweight expert models to extract spatio-temporal features and task-specific gating modules to adaptively weight experts across traits and stages. Evaluated on the PsyMo benchmark covering 17 psychological traits, our method outperforms state-of-the-art gait analysis models, achieving a 37.47% weighted F1 score at the run level and 44.6% at the subject level. Our experiments show that integrating auxiliary tasks such as identity recognition, gender prediction, and BMI estimation further improves psychological trait estimation. Our findings demonstrate the viability of multi-task gait-based learning for psychological trait estimation and provide a foundation for future research on movement-informed psychological inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 Figures, 4 Tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04654","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:46Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:27:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04093","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04093","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04093","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guixian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Guixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guan","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Guan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanmei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanmei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyun","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Zhenyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debo","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Debo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Harnessing LLM for Noise-Robust Cognitive Diagnosis in Web-Based Intelligent Education Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T08:32:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:51:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T09:32:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:36:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cognitive diagnostics in the Web-based Intelligent Education System (WIES) aims to assess students' mastery of knowledge concepts from heterogeneous, noisy interactions. Recent work has tried to utilize Large Language Models (LLMs) for cognitive diagnosis, yet LLMs struggle with structured data and are prone to noise-induced misjudgments. Specially, WIES's open environment continuously attracts new students and produces vast amounts of response logs, exacerbating the data imbalance and noise issues inherent in traditional educational systems. To address these challenges, we propose DLLM, a Diffusion-based LLM framework for noise-robust cognitive diagnosis. DLLM first constructs independent subgraphs based on response correctness, then applies relation augmentation alignment module to mitigate data imbalance. The two subgraph representations are then fused and aligned with LLM-derived, semantically augmented representations. Importantly, before each alignment step, DLLM employs a two-stage denoising diffusion module to eliminate intrinsic noise while assisting structural representation alignment. Specifically, unconditional denoising diffusion first removes erroneous information, followed by conditional denoising diffusion based on graph-guided to eliminate misleading information. Finally, the noise-robust representation that integrates semantic knowledge and structural information is fed into existing cognitive diagnosis models for prediction. Experimental results on three publicly available web-based educational platform datasets demonstrate that our DLLM achieves optimal predictive performance across varying noise levels, which demonstrates that DLLM achieves noise robustness while effectively leveraging semantic knowledge from LLM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04093","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:24:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03318","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03318","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03318","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Kabil","name":"Kabil, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ghada","familyName":"Khoriba","name":"Khoriba, Ghada","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mina","familyName":"Yousef","name":"Yousef, Mina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Essam A.","familyName":"Rashed","name":"Rashed, Essam A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Advances in Medical Image Segmentation: A Comprehensive Survey with a Focus on Lumbar Spine Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T01:34:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:20:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.compbiomed.2025.111171","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical Image Segmentation (MIS) stands as a cornerstone in medical image analysis, playing a pivotal role in precise diagnostics, treatment planning, and monitoring of various medical conditions. This paper presents a comprehensive and systematic survey of MIS methodologies, bridging the gap between traditional image processing techniques and modern deep learning approaches. The survey encompasses thresholding, edge detection, region-based segmentation, clustering algorithms, and model-based techniques while also delving into state-of-the-art deep learning architectures such as Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), Fully Convolutional Networks (FCNs), and the widely adopted U-Net and its variants. Moreover, integrating attention mechanisms, semi-supervised learning, generative adversarial networks (GANs), and Transformer-based models is thoroughly explored. In addition to covering established methods, this survey highlights emerging trends, including hybrid architectures, cross-modality learning, federated and distributed learning frameworks, and active learning strategies, which aim to address challenges such as limited labeled datasets, computational complexity, and model generalizability across diverse imaging modalities. Furthermore, a specialized case study on lumbar spine segmentation is presented, offering insights into the challenges and advancements in this relatively underexplored anatomical region. Despite significant progress in the field, critical challenges persist, including dataset bias, domain adaptation, interpretability of deep learning models, and integration into real-world clinical workflows."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Computers in Biology and Medicine (to appear)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03318","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:01Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:21:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04797","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04797","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04797","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuwen","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Shuwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julien","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Julien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingzi","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xingzi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehmet Saygin","familyName":"Seyfioglu","name":"Seyfioglu, Mehmet Saygin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kee Kiat","familyName":"Koo","name":"Koo, Kee Kiat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karim","familyName":"Bouyarmane","name":"Bouyarmane, Karim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiT-VTON: Diffusion Transformer Framework for Unified Multi-Category Virtual Try-On and Virtual Try-All with Integrated Image Editing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T16:27:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:33:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid growth of e-commerce has intensified the demand for Virtual Try-On (VTO) technologies, enabling customers to realistically visualize products overlaid on their own images. Despite recent advances, existing VTO models face challenges with fine-grained detail preservation, robustness to real-world imagery, efficient sampling, image editing capabilities, and generalization across diverse product categories. In this paper, we present DiT-VTON, a novel VTO framework that leverages a Diffusion Transformer (DiT), renowned for its performance on text-conditioned image generation, adapted here for the image-conditioned VTO task. We systematically explore multiple DiT configurations, including in-context token concatenation, channel concatenation, and ControlNet integration, to determine the best setup for VTO image conditioning.\n To enhance robustness, we train the model on an expanded dataset encompassing varied backgrounds, unstructured references, and non-garment categories, demonstrating the benefits of data scaling for VTO adaptability. DiT-VTON also redefines the VTO task beyond garment try-on, offering a versatile Virtual Try-All (VTA) solution capable of handling a wide range of product categories and supporting advanced image editing functionalities such as pose preservation, localized editing, texture transfer, and object-level customization. Experimental results show that our model surpasses state-of-the-art methods on VITON-HD, achieving superior detail preservation and robustness without reliance on additional condition encoders. It also outperforms models with VTA and image editing capabilities on a diverse dataset spanning thousands of product categories."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to CVPR 2025 and Published at CVPR 2025 AI for Content Creation workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04797","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:23:20Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:23:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:23:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04796","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04796","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04796","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samah","familyName":"Kansab","name":"Kansab, Samah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francis","familyName":"Bordeleau","name":"Bordeleau, Francis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Tizghadam","name":"Tizghadam, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RevMine: An LLM-Assisted Tool for Code Review Mining and Analysis Across Git Platforms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:22:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:33:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Empirical research on code review processes is increasingly central to understanding software quality and collaboration. However, collecting and analyzing review data remains a time-consuming and technically intensive task. Most researchers follow similar workflows - writing ad hoc scripts to extract, filter, and analyze review data from platforms like GitHub and GitLab. This paper introduces RevMine, a conceptual tool that streamlines the entire code review mining pipeline using large language models (LLMs). RevMine guides users through authentication, endpoint discovery, and natural language-driven data collection, significantly reducing the need for manual scripting. After retrieving review data, it supports both quantitative and qualitative analysis based on user-defined filters or LLM-inferred patterns. This poster outlines the tool's architecture, use cases, and research potential. By lowering the barrier to entry, RevMine aims to democratize code review mining and enable a broader range of empirical software engineering studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04796","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:23:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:23:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:23:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04794","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04794","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04794","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alon","familyName":"Kaya","name":"Kaya, Alon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igal","familyName":"Bilik","name":"Bilik, Igal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Inna","familyName":"Stainvas","name":"Stainvas, Inna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Comparative Study of Vision Transformers and CNNs for Few-Shot Rigid Transformation and Fundamental Matrix Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:18:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:33:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-transformers (ViTs) and large-scale convolution-neural-networks (CNNs) have reshaped computer vision through pretrained feature representations that enable strong transfer learning for diverse tasks. However, their efficiency as backbone architectures for geometric estimation tasks involving image deformations in low-data regimes remains an open question. This work considers two such tasks: 1) estimating 2D rigid transformations between pairs of images and 2) predicting the fundamental matrix for stereo image pairs, an important problem in various applications, such as autonomous mobility, robotics, and 3D scene reconstruction. Addressing this intriguing question, this work systematically compares large-scale CNNs (ResNet, EfficientNet, CLIP-ResNet) with ViT-based foundation models (CLIP-ViT variants and DINO) in various data size settings, including few-shot scenarios. These pretrained models are optimized for classification or contrastive learning, encouraging them to focus mostly on high-level semantics. The considered tasks require balancing local and global features differently, challenging the straightforward adoption of these models as the backbone. Empirical comparative analysis shows that, similar to training from scratch, ViTs outperform CNNs during refinement in large downstream-data scenarios. However, in small data scenarios, the inductive bias and smaller capacity of CNNs improve their performance, allowing them to match that of a ViT. Moreover, ViTs exhibit stronger generalization in cross-domain evaluation where the data distribution changes. These results emphasize the importance of carefully selecting model architectures for refinement, motivating future research towards hybrid architectures that balance local and global representations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04794","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:23:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:23:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:23:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04791","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04791","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04791","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristian","familyName":"Kolthoff","name":"Kolthoff, Kristian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Kretzer","name":"Kretzer, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone Paolo","familyName":"Ponzetto","name":"Ponzetto, Simone Paolo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Maedche","name":"Maedche, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Bartelt","name":"Bartelt, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GUISpector: An MLLM Agent Framework for Automated Verification of Natural Language Requirements in GUI Prototypes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:15:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:32:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"GUIs are foundational to interactive systems and play a pivotal role in early requirements elicitation through prototyping. Ensuring that GUI implementations fulfill NL requirements is essential for robust software engineering, especially as LLM-driven programming agents become increasingly integrated into development workflows. Existing GUI testing approaches, whether traditional or LLM-driven, often fall short in handling the complexity of modern interfaces, and typically lack actionable feedback and effective integration with automated development agents. In this paper, we introduce GUISpector, a novel framework that leverages a multi-modal (M)LLM-based agent for the automated verification of NL requirements in GUI prototypes. First, GUISpector adapts a MLLM agent to interpret and operationalize NL requirements, enabling to autonomously plan and execute verification trajectories across GUI applications. Second, GUISpector systematically extracts detailed NL feedback from the agent's verification process, providing developers with actionable insights that can be used to iteratively refine the GUI artifact or directly inform LLM-based code generation in a closed feedback loop. Third, we present an integrated tool that unifies these capabilities, offering practitioners an accessible interface for supervising verification runs, inspecting agent rationales and managing the end-to-end requirements verification process. We evaluated GUISpector on a comprehensive set of 150 requirements based on 900 acceptance criteria annotations across diverse GUI applications, demonstrating effective detection of requirement satisfaction and violations and highlighting its potential for seamless integration of actionable feedback into automated LLM-driven development workflows. The video presentation of GUISpector is available at: https://youtu.be/JByYF6BNQeE, showcasing its main capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04791","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:23:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:23:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:23:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04765","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04765","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04765","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinbo","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Jinbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoying","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xiaoying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianhang","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jianhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dusit","familyName":"Niyato","name":"Niyato, Dusit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:39:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:31:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Web 3.0 represents the next generation of the Internet, which is widely recognized as a decentralized ecosystem that focuses on value expression and data ownership. By leveraging blockchain and artificial intelligence technologies, Web 3.0 offers unprecedented opportunities for users to create, own, and monetize their content, thereby enabling User-Generated Content (UGC) to an entirely new level. However, some self-interested users may exploit the limitations of content curation mechanisms and generate low-quality content with less effort, obtaining platform rewards under information asymmetry. Such behavior can undermine Web 3.0 performance. To this end, we propose \\textit{LMM-Incentive}, a novel Large Multimodal Model (LMM)-based incentive mechanism for UGC in Web 3.0. Specifically, we propose an LMM-based contract-theoretic model to motivate users to generate high-quality UGC, thereby mitigating the adverse selection problem from information asymmetry. To alleviate potential moral hazards after contract selection, we leverage LMM agents to evaluate UGC quality, which is the primary component of the contract, utilizing prompt engineering techniques to improve the evaluation performance of LMM agents. Recognizing that traditional contract design methods cannot effectively adapt to the dynamic environment of Web 3.0, we develop an improved Mixture of Experts (MoE)-based Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) algorithm for optimal contract design. Simulation results demonstrate the superiority of the proposed MoE-based PPO algorithm over representative benchmarks in the context of contract design. Finally, we deploy the designed contract within an Ethereum smart contract framework, further validating the effectiveness of the proposed scheme."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04765","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:22:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04757","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04757","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04757","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduardo Martínez","familyName":"Rivera","name":"Rivera, Eduardo Martínez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filippo","familyName":"Menolascina","name":"Menolascina, Filippo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ModernBERT + ColBERT: Enhancing biomedical RAG through an advanced re-ranking retriever"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:34:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:30:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is a powerful technique for enriching Large Language Models (LLMs) with external knowledge, allowing for factually grounded responses, a critical requirement in high-stakes domains such as healthcare. However, the efficacy of RAG systems is fundamentally restricted by the performance of their retrieval module, since irrelevant or semantically misaligned documents directly compromise the accuracy of the final generated response. General-purpose dense retrievers can struggle with the nuanced language of specialised domains, while the high accuracy of in-domain models is often achieved at prohibitive computational costs. In this work, we aim to address this trade-off by developing and evaluating a two-stage retrieval architecture that combines a lightweight ModernBERT bidirectional encoder for efficient initial candidate retrieval with a ColBERTv2 late-interaction model for fine-grained re-ranking. We conduct comprehensive evaluations of our retriever module performance and RAG system performance in the biomedical context, fine-tuning the IR module using 10k question-passage pairs from PubMedQA. Our analysis of the retriever module confirmed the positive impact of the ColBERT re-ranker, which improved Recall@3 by up to 4.2 percentage points compared to its retrieve-only counterpart. When integrated into the biomedical RAG, our IR module leads to a state-of-the-art average accuracy of 0.4448 on the five tasks of the MIRAGE question-answering benchmark, outperforming strong baselines such as MedCPT (0.4436). Our ablation studies reveal that this performance is critically dependent on a joint fine-tuning process that aligns the retriever and re-ranker; otherwise, the re-ranker might degrade the performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04757","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:22:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04753","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04753","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04753","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masoumeh","familyName":"Chapariniya","name":"Chapariniya, Masoumeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teodora","familyName":"Vukovic","name":"Vukovic, Teodora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Ebling","name":"Ebling, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Volker","familyName":"Dellwo","name":"Dellwo, Volker","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Appearance: Transformer-based Person Identification from Conversational Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:31:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:30:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates the performance of transformer-based architectures for person identification in natural, face-to-face conversation scenario. We implement and evaluate a two-stream framework that separately models spatial configurations and temporal motion patterns of 133 COCO WholeBody keypoints, extracted from a subset of the CANDOR conversational corpus. Our experiments compare pre-trained and from-scratch training, investigate the use of velocity features, and introduce a multi-scale temporal transformer for hierarchical motion modeling. Results demonstrate that domain-specific training significantly outperforms transfer learning, and that spatial configurations carry more discriminative information than temporal dynamics. The spatial transformer achieves 95.74% accuracy, while the multi-scale temporal transformer achieves 93.90%. Feature-level fusion pushes performance to 98.03%, confirming that postural and dynamic information are complementary. These findings highlight the potential of transformer architectures for person identification in natural interactions and provide insights for future multimodal and cross-cultural studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04753","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:13Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:22:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04749","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04749","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04749","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samy","familyName":"Ateia","name":"Ateia, Samy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Udo","familyName":"Kruschwitz","name":"Kruschwitz, Udo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Melanie","familyName":"Scholz","name":"Scholz, Melanie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agnes","familyName":"Koschmider","name":"Koschmider, Agnes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moayad","familyName":"Almohaishi","name":"Almohaishi, Moayad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Based Information Extraction to Support Scientific Literature Research and Publication Workflows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:27:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:30:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-06136-2_9","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The increasing volume of scholarly publications requires advanced tools for efficient knowledge discovery and management. This paper introduces ongoing work on a system using Large Language Models (LLMs) for the semantic extraction of key concepts from scientific documents. Our research, conducted within the German National Research Data Infrastructure for and with Computer Science (NFDIxCS) project, seeks to support FAIR (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable) principles in scientific publishing. We outline our explorative work, which uses in-context learning with various LLMs to extract concepts from papers, initially focusing on the Business Process Management (BPM) domain. A key advantage of this approach is its potential for rapid domain adaptation, often requiring few or even zero examples to define extraction targets for new scientific fields. We conducted technical evaluations to compare the performance of commercial and open-source LLMs and created an online demo application to collect feedback from an initial user-study. Additionally, we gathered insights from the computer science research community through user stories collected during a dedicated workshop, actively guiding the ongoing development of our future services. These services aim to support structured literature reviews, concept-based information retrieval, and integration of extracted knowledge into existing knowledge graphs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This PDF is the author-prepared camera-ready version corresponding to the accepted manuscript and supersedes the submitted version that was inadvertently published as the version of record"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04749","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:22:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04721","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04721","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04721","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivo","familyName":"Petrov","name":"Petrov, Ivo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jasper","familyName":"Dekoninck","name":"Dekoninck, Jasper","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Vechev","name":"Vechev, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BrokenMath: A Benchmark for Sycophancy in Theorem Proving with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T11:41:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:29:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have recently shown strong performance on mathematical benchmarks. At the same time, they are prone to hallucination and sycophancy, often providing convincing but flawed proofs for incorrect mathematical statements provided by users. This significantly limits the applicability of LLMs in theorem proving, as verification of these flawed proofs must be done manually by expert mathematicians. However, existing benchmarks that measure sycophancy in mathematics are limited: they focus solely on final-answer problems, rely on very simple and often contaminated datasets, and construct benchmark samples using synthetic modifications that create ill-posed questions rather than well-posed questions that are demonstrably false. To address these issues, we introduce BrokenMath, the first benchmark for evaluating sycophantic behavior in LLMs within the context of natural language theorem proving. BrokenMath is built from advanced 2025 competition problems, which are perturbed with an LLM to produce false statements and subsequently refined through expert review. Using an LLM-as-a-judge framework, we evaluate state-of-the-art LLMs and agentic systems and find that sycophancy is widespread, with the best model, GPT-5, producing sycophantic answers 29% of the time. We further investigate several mitigation strategies, including test-time interventions and supervised fine-tuning on curated sycophantic examples. These approaches substantially reduce, but do not eliminate, sycophantic behavior."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04721","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:21:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:21:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:21:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04717","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04717","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04717","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarel","familyName":"Duanis","name":"Duanis, Sarel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asnat","familyName":"Greenstein-Messica","name":"Greenstein-Messica, Asnat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eliya","familyName":"Habba","name":"Habba, Eliya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JSON Whisperer: Efficient JSON Editing with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T11:36:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:28:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) can modify JSON documents through natural language commands, but current approaches regenerate entire structures for each edit, resulting in computational inefficiency. We present JSON Whisperer, a framework that enables LLMs to generate RFC 6902 diff patches-expressing only the necessary modifications-rather than complete documents. We identify two key challenges in patch-based editing: (1) LLMs often miss related updates when generating isolated patches, and (2) array manipulations require tracking index shifts across operations, which LLMs handle poorly. To address these issues, we introduce EASE (Explicitly Addressed Sequence Encoding), which transforms arrays into dictionaries with stable keys, eliminating index arithmetic complexities. Our evaluation shows that patch generation with EASE reduces token usage by 31% while maintaining edit quality within 5% of full regeneration with particular gains for complex instructions and list manipulations. The dataset is available at: https://github.com/emnlp2025/JSON-Whisperer/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04717","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:21:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:21:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:21:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zexin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changhua","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Changhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyue","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Hengyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Si","name":"Si, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianhui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaogang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Gaogang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jingjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ViTs: Teaching Machines to See Time Series Anomalies Like Human Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T11:24:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:28:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Web service administrators must ensure the stability of multiple systems by promptly detecting anomalies in Key Performance Indicators (KPIs). Achieving the goal of \"train once, infer across scenarios\" remains a fundamental challenge for time series anomaly detection models. Beyond improving zero-shot generalization, such models must also flexibly handle sequences of varying lengths during inference, ranging from one hour to one week, without retraining. Conventional approaches rely on sliding-window encoding and self-supervised learning, which restrict inference to fixed-length inputs. Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable zero-shot capabilities across general domains. However, when applied to time series data, they face inherent limitations due to context length. To address this issue, we propose ViTs, a Vision-Language Model (VLM)-based framework that converts time series curves into visual representations. By rescaling time series images, temporal dependencies are preserved while maintaining a consistent input size, thereby enabling efficient processing of arbitrarily long sequences without context constraints. Training VLMs for this purpose introduces unique challenges, primarily due to the scarcity of aligned time series image-text data. To overcome this, we employ an evolutionary algorithm to automatically generate thousands of high-quality image-text pairs and design a three-stage training pipeline consisting of: (1) time series knowledge injection, (2) anomaly detection enhancement, and (3) anomaly reasoning refinement. Extensive experiments demonstrate that ViTs substantially enhance the ability of VLMs to understand and detect anomalies in time series data. All datasets and code will be publicly released at: https://anonymous.4open.science/r/ViTs-C484/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:21:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:21:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:21:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04695","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04695","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04695","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiding","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhepei","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zhepei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Outcome Reward: Decoupling Search and Answering Improves LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T11:09:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:27:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Enabling large language models (LLMs) to utilize search tools offers a promising path to overcoming fundamental limitations such as knowledge cutoffs and hallucinations. Recent work has explored reinforcement learning (RL) for training search-augmented agents that interleave reasoning and retrieval before answering. These approaches usually rely on outcome-based rewards (e.g., exact match), implicitly assuming that optimizing for final answers will also yield effective intermediate search behaviors. Our analysis challenges this assumption: we uncover multiple systematic deficiencies in search that arise under outcome-only training and ultimately degrade final answer quality, including failure to invoke tools, invalid queries, and redundant searches. To address these shortcomings, we introduce DeSA (Decoupling Search-and-Answering), a simple two-stage training framework that explicitly separates search optimization from answer generation. In Stage 1, agents are trained to improve search effectiveness with retrieval recall-based rewards. In Stage 2, outcome rewards are employed to optimize final answer generation. Across seven QA benchmarks, DeSA-trained agents consistently improve search behaviors, delivering substantially higher search recall and answer accuracy than outcome-only baselines. Notably, DeSA outperforms single-stage training approaches that simultaneously optimize recall and outcome rewards, underscoring the necessity of explicitly decoupling the two objectives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04695","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:20:45Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:20:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:20:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04678","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04678","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04678","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanfeng","familyName":"Mo","name":"Mo, Zhanfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingxuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lidong","familyName":"Bing","name":"Bing, Lidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Agent Tool-Integrated Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T10:44:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:26:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) increasingly rely on multi-turn tool-integrated planning for knowledge-intensive and complex reasoning tasks. Existing implementations typically rely on a single agent, but they suffer from limited context length and noisy tool responses. A natural solution is to adopt a multi-agent framework with planner- and worker-agents to manage context. However, no existing methods support effective reinforcement learning post-training of tool-integrated multi-agent frameworks. To address this gap, we propose Multi-Agent Tool-Integrated Policy Optimization (MATPO), which enables distinct roles (planner and worker) to be trained within a single LLM instance using role-specific prompts via reinforcement learning. MATPO is derived from a principled credit assignment mechanism across planner and worker rollouts. This design eliminates the need to deploy multiple LLMs, which would be memory-intensive, while preserving the benefits of specialization. Experiments on GAIA-text, WebWalkerQA, and FRAMES show that MATPO consistently outperforms single-agent baselines by an average of 18.38% relative improvement in performance and exhibits greater robustness to noisy tool outputs. Our findings highlight the effectiveness of unifying multiple agent roles within a single LLM and provide practical insights for stable and efficient multi-agent RL training."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04678","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:20:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:20:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:20:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04671","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04671","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04671","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tengxiao","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Tengxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lejing","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Lejing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hongfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FocusMed: A Large Language Model-based Framework for Enhancing Medical Question Summarization with Focus Identification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T10:27:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:25:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid development of online medical platforms, consumer health questions (CHQs) are inefficient in diagnosis due to redundant information and frequent non-professional terms. The medical question summary (MQS) task aims to transform CHQs into streamlined doctors' frequently asked questions (FAQs), but existing methods still face challenges such as poor identification of question focus and model hallucination. This paper explores the potential of large language models (LLMs) in the MQS task and finds that direct fine-tuning is prone to focus identification bias and generates unfaithful content. To this end, we propose an optimization framework based on core focus guidance. First, a prompt template is designed to drive the LLMs to extract the core focus from the CHQs that is faithful to the original text. Then, a fine-tuning dataset is constructed in combination with the original CHQ-FAQ pairs to improve the ability to identify the focus of the question. Finally, a multi-dimensional quality evaluation and selection mechanism is proposed to comprehensively improve the quality of the summary from multiple dimensions. We conduct comprehensive experiments on two widely-adopted MQS datasets using three established evaluation metrics. The proposed framework achieves state-of-the-art performance across all measures, demonstrating a significant boost in the model's ability to identify critical focus of questions and a notable mitigation of hallucinations. The source codes are freely available at https://github.com/DUT-LiuChao/FocusMed."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a regular paper at BIBM2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04671","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:20:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:20:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:20:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04648","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04648","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04648","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Buyuan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Buyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiping","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang Hao","familyName":"Cheong","name":"Cheong, Kang Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EduPersona: Benchmarking Subjective Ability Boundaries of Virtual Student Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:52:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:23:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models are increasingly integrated into education, virtual student agents are becoming vital for classroom simulation and teacher training. Yet their classroom-oriented subjective abilities remain largely unassessed, limiting understanding of model boundaries and hindering trustworthy deployment. We present EduPersona, a large-scale benchmark spanning two languages, three subjects, and ten persona types based on the Big Five theory. The dataset contains 1,308 authentic classroom dialogue rounds, corresponding to 12,814 teacher-student Q\u0026amp;A turns, and is further expanded through persona stylization into roughly 10 times larger scale (128k turns), providing a solid foundation for evaluation. Building on this resource, we decompose hard-to-quantify subjective performance into three progressive tasks: TASK1 basic coherence (whether behavior, emotion, expression, and voice align with classroom context), TASK2 student realism, and TASK3 long-term persona consistency, thereby establishing an evaluation framework grounded in educational theory and research value. We conduct systematic experiments on three representative LLMs, comparing their original versions with ten persona-fine-tuned variants trained on EduPersona. Results show consistent and significant average improvements across all tasks: TASK1 +33.6%, TASK2 +30.6%, and TASK3 +14.9%. These improvements highlight the dataset's effectiveness and research value, while also revealing the heterogeneous difficulty of persona modeling. In summary, EduPersona delivers the first classroom benchmark centered on subjective abilities, establishes a decoupled and verifiable research paradigm, and we will open-source both the dataset and the framework to support the broader research community in advancing trustworthy and human-like AI for education."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint, Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04648","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:19:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04644","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04644","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04644","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hirotsugu","familyName":"Kakugawa","name":"Kakugawa, Hirotsugu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sayaka","familyName":"Kamei","name":"Kamei, Sayaka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masahiro","familyName":"Shibata","name":"Shibata, Masahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fukuhito","familyName":"Ooshita","name":"Ooshita, Fukuhito","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The R(1)W(1) Communication Model for Self-Stabilizing Distributed Algorithms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:45:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:23:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-stabilization is a versatile methodology in the design of fault-tolerant distributed algorithms for transient faults. A self-stabilizing system automatically recovers from any kind and any finite number of transient faults. This property is specifically useful in modern distributed systems with a large number of components. In this paper, we propose a new communication and execution model named the R(1)W(1) model in which each process can read and write its own and neighbors' local variables in a single step. We propose self-stabilizing distributed algorithms in the R(1)W(1) model for the problems of maximal matching, minimal k-dominating set and maximal k-dependent set. Finally, we propose an example transformer, based on randomized distance-two local mutual exclusion, to simulate algorithms designed for the R(1)W(1) model in the synchronous message passing model with synchronized clocks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04644","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:19:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04643","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04643","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04643","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yawen","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofen","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Xiaofen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangmin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiangmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"QuantAgents: Towards Multi-agent Financial System via Simulated Trading"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:45:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:23:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, our objective is to develop a multi-agent financial system that incorporates simulated trading, a technique extensively utilized by financial professionals. While current LLM-based agent models demonstrate competitive performance, they still exhibit significant deviations from real-world fund companies. A critical distinction lies in the agents' reliance on ``post-reflection'', particularly in response to adverse outcomes, but lack a distinctly human capability: long-term prediction of future trends. Therefore, we introduce QuantAgents, a multi-agent system integrating simulated trading, to comprehensively evaluate various investment strategies and market scenarios without assuming actual risks. Specifically, QuantAgents comprises four agents: a simulated trading analyst, a risk control analyst, a market news analyst, and a manager, who collaborate through several meetings. Moreover, our system incentivizes agents to receive feedback on two fronts: performance in real-world markets and predictive accuracy in simulated trading. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our framework excels across all metrics, yielding an overall return of nearly 300% over the three years (https://quantagents.github.io/)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted by EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04643","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:19:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04637","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04637","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04637","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanju","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yanju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heyuan","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Heyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tenglong","familyName":"Ao","name":"Ao, Tenglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohang","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Xiaohang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Libin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Libin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Social Agent: Mastering Dyadic Nonverbal Behavior Generation via Conversational LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:41:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:23:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3757377.3763879","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Social Agent, a novel framework for synthesizing realistic and contextually appropriate co-speech nonverbal behaviors in dyadic conversations. In this framework, we develop an agentic system driven by a Large Language Model (LLM) to direct the conversation flow and determine appropriate interactive behaviors for both participants. Additionally, we propose a novel dual-person gesture generation model based on an auto-regressive diffusion model, which synthesizes coordinated motions from speech signals. The output of the agentic system is translated into high-level guidance for the gesture generator, resulting in realistic movement at both the behavioral and motion levels. Furthermore, the agentic system periodically examines the movements of interlocutors and infers their intentions, forming a continuous feedback loop that enables dynamic and responsive interactions between the two participants. User studies and quantitative evaluations show that our model significantly improves the quality of dyadic interactions, producing natural, synchronized nonverbal behaviors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"SIGGRAPH ASIA 2025 (Conference Track); Project page: https://pku-mocca.github.io/Social-Agent-Page/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04637","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:19:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":372,"totalPages":4,"page":2},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-06T16%3A00%3A00Z%20TO%202025-10-07T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D%20AND%20(%22language%20model%22%20OR%20%22language%20models%22%20OR%20LLM%20OR%20LLMs%20OR%20transformer%20OR%20transformers%20OR%20%22mixture%20of%20experts%22)\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=3\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-06T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-07T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22%29"}}